hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"11496","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, or another — Abu Bakr was unsure — from Ibrahim, he said: When the cause comes from her side, what he took from her is lawful for him; if it comes from his side, what he took from her is not lawful for him."
"11497","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il, from al-Sha'bi, he said: When a woman hates her husband, what he took from her is lawful for him."
"11498","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I asked Ata about a man who wanted to divorce his wife, so he asked her to gift him part of her dower, and she did so willingly, then he divorced her. He said: I said to him, why so, when Allah the Exalted said, 'But if they willingly forgo part of it for you,' and he recited, 'And if you wish to replace one wife with another.'"
"11499","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ikrimah ibn Khalid informed me that a man of the family of Abu Mu'ayt — his wife gave him a thousand dinars, while he owed her a dower — then he waited a month, then divorced her, so she disputed with him before Abd al-Malik, while I was present. The divorcer said, 'She gave it to me willingly,' and Allah has said, 'But if they willingly forgo part of it for you.' Abd al-Malik said: Then where is the verse after it, 'And if you wish to replace one wife with another'? Return her thousand to her. So he ruled it for her against him, while I was present. Ibn Jurayj said: I was told she was A'ishah."
"11500","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, he said: A dispute was brought before Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan, while I was present, concerning a man whose wife forwent her dower for him, then he divorced her. A man in his presence said: Allah the Exalted has said, 'But if they willingly forgo part of it for you, consume it with wholesome pleasure.' Abd al-Malik said: Has not Allah said, 'And if you wish to replace one wife with another'? And he recited it. He said: So he returned her property to her. He said: And some of them said: If at the time he asked her he intended divorce, and he admitted it, then he returns her dower to her."
"11501","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, concerning a woman who willingly forwent something for her husband, then they remained together, then he divorced her after that, he said: It is valid for the husband, and she has no right to take it back."
"11502","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Shubrumah, he said: She is made to swear that she did not forgo it willingly, then he returns to her what she forwent for him."
"11503","From Ibn Uyaynah, from one who heard Mujahid, saying concerning Allah's saying, 'But if they willingly forgo part of it for you,' he said: Up to death."
"11504","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Ja'far, he said: I saw Shurayh, and a woman came disputing with her husband, and he claimed that she had absolved him of her dower. Shurayh said: Evidence — did you see the silver? They said: No, so he did not uphold it."
"11505","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata, a man separated from his wife by khul' though khul' was not rightful for him, and he stipulated, 'If you dispute me, you are my wife,' he said: It is one divorce, she has more right to her affair, and her property is to be returned to her. I said: Then where is his stipulation? He said: Allah's stipulation precedes his stipulation. He said: And he has divorced; khul' is divorce. He said: And he informed me, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz ruled thus, and I consider it nothing but an excellent ruling."
"11506","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: When a woman ransoms herself from her husband, and produces proof that the recalcitrance was on his side and that he was harming her and vexing her, her property is returned to her, divorce has taken effect between them, and she has more right to her affair."
"11507","From Qatadah, he said: If she disputed him during the waiting period and produced proof that he was harming her and treating her badly in companionship until she ransomed herself from him, her property is returned to her, and he retains the right of return over her. If the waiting period has passed, her property is returned to her, and she has more right to herself."
"11508","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: If he took her ransom though taking it was not lawful for him, her property is returned to her, she returns to him, and he loses neither her person nor her property."
"11509","From Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj, they both said: Ibn Tawus informed us, from his father, that he used to say: It is not lawful for him to take from her more than what he gave her."
"11510","From Amr ibn Hawshab, he said: I heard Tawus saying: It is not lawful for him to take from her more than what he gave her."
"11511","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata, a woman ransomed herself from her husband with an addition over her dower, he said: No; the addition is returned to her, even if her ransom was lawful for him and she gave it to him willingly. Mutual release is the same."
"11512","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Hasan ibn Muslim informed me, from Tawus, that he used to say: We do not deem it right for a man, even if his wife's khul' was valid for him, to take from her more than her dower."
"11513","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me, a woman came to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I hate my husband and love separation from him. He said: Will you return to him the garden he gave you as dower? And he had given her a garden as dower. She said: Yes, plus an addition from my property. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: As for an addition from your property, no; but the garden. She said: Yes. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ruled that against the man, and he was informed of the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ruling, and he said: I accept the ruling of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"11514","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas had with him the daughter of Abdullah ibn Salul, and he had given her a garden as dower, but she disliked him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Will you return to him the garden he gave you? She said: Yes. So he took it and released her. When that reached Thabit ibn Qays, he said: I accept the ruling of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu al-Zubayr heard it from more than one."
"11515","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, that Ali ibn Abi Talib said: He shall not take from her more than what he gave her. From Ma'mar, that it reached him the same from Ali."
"11516","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Karim, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: I would not like him to take from her all that he gave her, until he leaves her what she can live on."
"11517","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ibn al-Musayyab: He shall not take all that he gave her."
"11518","From Ma'mar, from one who heard al-Hasan, saying: He shall not take from her more than what he gave her."
"11519","From al-Thawri, from Abu Husayn, from al-Sha'bi, he said: I dislike that he take from her all that he gave her."
"11520","From Ma'mar, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib — may Allah ennoble his face — that al-Rubayyi' daughter of Mu'awwidh ibn Afra informed him, she said: I had a husband who showed me little good when present and deprived me when absent. She said: One day a lapse came from me, and I said to him: I will separate from you by khul' with all I own. He said: Yes. She said: So I did. Then my uncle Mu'adh ibn Afra disputed before Uthman, and he upheld the khul'. She said: And he ordered him to take my headband and what is below it — or she said: below the headband."
"11521","From Ma'mar, from Kathir, freedman of Samurah, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab seized a recalcitrant woman, admonished her, but she did not accept any good, so he confined her in a house full of dung for three days, then brought her out and said: What do you think? She said: O Commander of the Faithful, by Allah I found no rest except these three days. Umar said: Separate from her by khul', woe to you, even for her earring."
"11522","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', that a freedwoman of Ibn Umar ransomed herself with everything except her breastplate, and he did not blame her for it."
"11523","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi', that a freedwoman of Ibn Umar's wife came to Ibn Umar, having ransomed herself with all she owned and every garment upon her, even herself, and Abdullah did not disapprove of that."
"11524","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that he heard Ikrimah, freedman of Ibn Abbas, saying: He may take from her even her earring."
"11525","From Ma'mar, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: Khul' is what is below the headband."
"11526","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: Khul' is what is below the headband, and a woman may indeed ransom herself with part of her property."
"11527","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: Let him take from her even her wraps."
"11528","From Ma'mar, from Amr ibn Muslim, from Ikrimah, freedman of Ibn Abbas, he said: The wife of Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas separated from her husband by khul', and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fixed her waiting period as one menstruation."
"11529","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', that Mu'adh ibn Afra married his brother's daughter to a man who drank wine, so Abdullah raised that to Uthman, and he upheld it, and ordered her to observe a waiting period of one menstruation."
"11530","From Isra'il, from Abd al-A'la, from Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, he said: The waiting period of a woman separated by khul' is like that of a divorced woman."
"11531","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, they said: Three menstrual cycles. Ma'mar said: Al-Hasan said it, and the people follow it."
"11532","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, he said: The waiting period of a woman separated by khul' is three menstrual cycles."
"11533","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, he said: The maintenance of a pregnant woman who ransomed herself is upon her husband. He said: Ibn Shihab said it, and Ibn Jurayj said: Whether he knew of her pregnancy or not, the maintenance is upon him, unless he stipulated that your maintenance is not upon me. Amr ibn Dinar said: He spends upon her; he is but spending upon his child."
"11534","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father, he said: She is entitled to maintenance."
"11535","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, concerning the maintenance of a pregnant woman who ransomed herself, he said: She is entitled to lodging and maintenance, unless he stipulates that you have no maintenance. Ibrahim said: His stipulation is valid for maintenance, but not valid for lodging."
"11536","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyab, concerning a pregnant woman separated by khul', he said: She is entitled to maintenance. Ma'mar said: Al-Zuhri used to hold the view of Ibn al-Musayyab on it, and say: She is also entitled to compensation."
"11537","From Uthman, from Sa'id, from Asim ibn al-Ahwal, from al-Sha'bi, he said: She is entitled to maintenance."
"11538","From Uthman, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, that Shurayh, Abu al-Aliyah and Khilas ibn Amr said: She is entitled to maintenance. He said: And Jabir ibn Abdullah and al-Hasan said: She has no maintenance."
"11539","Ma'mar, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, concerning a pregnant woman separated by khul', if he did not stipulate, then maintenance is due to her."
"11540","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, he said: I said: Did you hear your father fix any limit for desertion? He said: No."
"11541","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that A'ishah said to Sa'id ibn al-As: Beware of prolonged desertion, for you know what Allah has fixed in ila': four months."
"11542","From Abdullah ibn Muharrar, from Yazid ibn al-Asamm, that Ibn Abbas said to him: How is Tahallul? My last knowledge of her was that she was sharp-tongued. He said: Yes, by Allah, I left while not speaking to her. He said: Hasten the journey before four months pass, for if four months pass, it is one irrevocable divorce, and you are a suitor."
"11543","From Ma'mar, from Ja'far ibn Burqan, from Yazid ibn al-Asamm, that Ibn Abbas said to him: How is Tahallul? My last knowledge of her was that she was ill-tempered. He said: Yes, by Allah, I left while not speaking to her. He said: Reach her before four months pass."
"11544","From al-Thawri, from a man, from Abu Salih, from Ibn Abbas, concerning His saying, 'And desert them,' he said: He deserts her with his tongue and is harsh with her in speech, but does not abandon intercourse with her."
"11545","From al-Thawri, from Khusayf, from Ikrimah, he said: Desertion is only by speech, that he is harsh with her, and not by intercourse."
"11546","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, concerning His saying, 'And beat them,' he said: He beats a beating that is not severe."
"11547","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Tawus, concerning His saying, 'And beat them,' he said: We heard that it is a beating that is not severe."
"11548","From al-Thawri, he said: Our companions said, he begins by admonishing her; if she accepts, well, otherwise he deserts her with his tongue and is harsh with her in that; if she accepts, well, otherwise he beats her a beating that is not severe. Then, 'If they obey you' — she comes to the bed while hating you — 'then seek no way against them.'"
"11549","From Ibn Jurayj, from Mujahid or another, he said: The pretexts."
"11550","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata — a man said to him: May the two arbiters separate them? He said: No, unless the couple places that in their hands."
"11551","From Ma'mar, from one who heard al-Hasan, saying: They may judge for reunion, but they may not judge for separation."
"11552","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: If the two arbiters wish to separate, they separate, and if they wish to join, they join."
"11553","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Ubaydah al-Salmani, he said: I witnessed Ali ibn Abi Talib, when a woman and her husband came to him, with each of them a group of people, and these brought forth an arbiter from the people, and those brought forth an arbiter. Ali said to the two arbiters: Do you know what is upon you? If you see fit to separate, you separate, and if you see fit to join, you join. The husband said: As for separation, no. Ali said: You lie; by Allah you shall not move until you accept the Book of Allah for you and against you. The woman said: I accept the Book of Allah the Exalted for me and against me."
"11554","From al-Thawri, from Jabir and others, from al-Sha'bi, he said: If the two arbiters wish, they separate, and if they wish, they join."
"11555","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I and Mu'awiyah were sent as two arbiters, and it was said to us, if you see fit to join, join, and if you see fit to separate, separate. Ma'mar said: And it reached me that the one who sent them was Uthman."
"11556","From Ma'mar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, he said: If the two arbiters wish to separate, they separate, and if they wish to join, they join."
"11557","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Abi Mulaykah narrated to me that Aqil ibn Abi Talib married Fatimah bint Utbah ibn Rabi'ah, and she said: Endure for me and I will spend upon you. When he entered upon her, she would say: Where are Utbah ibn Rabi'ah and Shaybah ibn Rabi'ah? And he would remain silent toward her, until one day he entered upon her while irritated, and she said: Where are Utbah ibn Rabi'ah and Shaybah ibn Rabi'ah? He said: To your left in the Fire, when you enter. So she wrapped her garments upon her and came to Uthman and mentioned that to him, and he laughed. He sent for Ibn Abbas and Mu'awiyah. Ibn Abbas said: I shall surely separate them. Mu'awiyah said: I would never separate two elders of Banu Abd Manaf. They came and found that they had locked their doors upon themselves and set their affair right, so they returned."
"11558","From Abdullah ibn Kathir, from Shu'bah ibn al-Hajjaj, he said: Amr ibn Murrah informed me, he said: I asked Sa'id ibn Jubayr about the two arbiters, and he became angry and said: I was not even born at that time. He said: So I said: I mean only the arbiters of discord. He said: When there is contention between a man and his wife, they send two arbiters; they turn to the one from whose side the contention arose and admonish him; if he obeys them, well, otherwise they turn to the other; if he listens to them and responds to what they want, well, otherwise whatever they judge between them is valid."
"11559","From al-Thawri, from Abu Hashim, from Mujahid, he said: 'If they both desire reconciliation' — the two arbiters — 'Allah will bring concord between them' — between the two arbiters."
"11560","From Ma'mar, he said: A woman came to al-Hasan and said: O Abu Sa'id, by Allah, Allah has created nothing more hated to me than my husband, while he imagines there is nothing on earth more beloved to me than him. Do you command me to seek khul'? Al-Hasan said: We used to narrate that women who seek khul' are the hypocrites. He said: So she struck her head with her hand and said: Then I shall endure upon Allah's blessing, the Exalted. Al-Hasan said: May Allah have mercy on her; I did not think she would do it."
"11561","From al-Thawri, from al-Ash'ath, raising it to the Prophet, he said: Women who seek khul' and those who wrest themselves away are the hypocrites."
"11562","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, raising the hadith to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Any woman who asks her husband for divorce without any harm shall not find the fragrance of Paradise — or he said: Allah has forbidden her to find the fragrance of Paradise."
"11563","From al-Thawri, from Ayyub and Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilabah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any woman who asks her husband for divorce without harm, the fragrance of Paradise is forbidden to her."
"11564","From Ibn Jurayj, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Ibn al-Musayyab, concerning a man who grants his wife authority over her affair, he said: If she returns her affair to him, it is nothing; if she accepts her affair, it is according to what she decrees."
"11565","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ata informed me that Hafsah bint Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr was with al-Mundhir ibn al-Zubayr, and something occurred between them, so A'ishah, Mother of the Believers, asked him to grant her authority over her affair. A'ishah presented that to Hafsah, but she refused to part from him. So A'ishah returned it to al-Mundhir, and nothing was counted."
"11566","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr informing from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, he said: Hayyah was with Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, and Quraybah bint Abi Umayyah, and he made them jealous of one another. Umm Salamah said: None married us but A'ishah, though the husband is Abd al-Rahman, and none overpowers us but through A'ishah. So A'ishah asked her brother to place Quraybah's affair in Quraybah's hand, and he did. A'ishah sent word of that to Umm Salamah. Umm Salamah said to her sister: As for A'ishah, she has completed her term, but as for you, decide in your affair what you will. She said: I return my affair to my husband. So nothing was counted. Abdullah said: And al-Qasim mentioned that it is narrated as one return to her husband from Ali."
"11567","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who grants his wife authority over her affair and she returns it to him, he said: It is nothing."
"11568","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: If she divorces herself, the decree is what she decreed: if one, then one; if two, then two; if three, then three."
"11569","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, that two men placed the affair of their wives in their hands, and they returned the affair to them, and the people counted that as nothing."
"11570","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from A'ishah, that she married Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr — or the son of Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, her nephew — to Quraybah daughter of Abu Umayyah, and something occurred between them. Her family said: By Allah, none married us but A'ishah. It reached her and they informed him, and he said: Her affair is in her hand. She said: By Allah, I shall not prefer anyone over him. Al-Qasim said: The people counted that as nothing."
"11571","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata, a woman owned her affair and returned it to her husband, he said: It is nothing. If she divorces herself, it is according to that: if one, then one; if two, then two; if three, then three."
"11572","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah and Ayyub, from Ghaylan ibn Jarir, from Abu al-Halal al-Ataki, that he came as a delegate to Uthman and asked him about things, among them a man who placed his wife's affair in her hand, and he said: It is in her hand."
"11573","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyab, they said: When a man grants his wife authority over her affair, the decree is what she decrees: if one, then one; if two, then two; if three, then three. Qatadah said: If she returns it to her husband, it is one divorce, and he has more right to her."
"11574","From Ibn Jurayj, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Ibn al-Musayyab, concerning a man who grants his wife authority, he said: If she returns her affair, it is nothing; if she accepts her affair, it is according to what she decrees."
"11575","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: When a man grants his wife authority over her affair, the decree is what she decrees; if he disputes her, he is made to swear. And he used to say: If she returns it to him, it is nothing. From Ma'mar and Ibn Jurayj, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, the same."
"11576","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, he said: I heard al-Harith ibn Abdullah saying: Any woman whose affair is placed in her hand or in her guardian's hand, and she divorces herself with three divorces, she is free of him. From Raja ibn Haywah, that Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan ruled thus."
"11577","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that a man placed his wife's affair in her hand, and she divorced herself three times. He asked Ibn Umar, who said: What is your name? He said: Mahr. He said: Mahr the fool; you deliberately took what Allah placed in your hand and placed it in her hand, and she is separated from you."
"11578","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from al-Hakam, from Ali, he said: When he places her affair in her hand, the decree is what she decrees, she and another are alike."
"11579","From Ma'mar, from Khallad ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: One who asked Ibn Umar informed me, concerning a man who granted his wife authority over her affair, and she divorced herself three times, and he said: She is divorced, despite his nose."
"11580","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, that Ibn Umar said: Whoever grants his wife authority, she is divorced, and he has disobeyed his Lord. Ma'mar said: And one who heard al-Hasan informed me that he said the same."
"11581","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Tawus informed me, from his father — and I said to him: How did your father speak concerning a man who grants his wife authority over her affair? May she divorce herself? He said: No; he used to say: Divorce is not in women's hands."
"11582","Al-Thawri informed us, from Mansur, he said: Ibrahim narrated to me, from Alqamah or al-Aswad, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: A man came to him and said: Between me and my wife was something of what occurs between people, and she said: If what is in your hand of my affair were in my hand, you would know what I would do. He said: What is in my hand of your affair is in your hand. She said: Then you are divorced three times. He said: I consider it one, and you have more right to return. I shall meet the Commander of the Faithful, Umar. So he met him and told him the story. He said: He said: May Allah deal with men, may Allah deal with men — they deliberately take what is in their hands and place it in women's hands; dust in her mouth. What did you say? He said: I said: I consider it one, and he has more right to her. He said: And I hold that view; had you held another view, I would have deemed you mistaken. Mansur said: So I said to Ibrahim, yet Ibn Abbas says: May Allah confound her omen — had she said, 'I divorced myself.' Ibrahim said: They are alike."
"11583","From al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, that a man placed his wife's affair in her hand and she divorced herself. Umar asked Ibn Mas'ud about her, what do you hold? He said: I consider it one, and he has more right to her. Umar said: And I hold that view."
"11584","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, from Abd al-Karim Abu Umayyah, that a Muslim man placed his wife's affair in her hand in the time of Umar ibn al-Khattab, and she divorced herself three times. The man said: By Allah, I placed your affair in your hand only for one. They both brought the case to Umar, and Umar made him swear by Allah, besides whom there is no god, that you placed her affair in her hand only for one. He swore, so he returned her to him."
"11585","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that he said concerning a man who placed his wife's affair in her hand, and she divorced herself three times, he said: It is one."
"11586","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed us that Mujahid informed him that a man came to Ibn Abbas and said: When I granted my wife authority over her affair, she divorced me three times. He said: May Allah confound her omen; divorce is yours over her, not hers over you."
"11587","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, that a woman whose husband granted her authority over her affair said: You are divorced, you are divorced. Ibn Abbas said: May Allah confound her omen; divorce is yours over her, not hers over you."
"11588","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Abbas, he said: May Allah confound her omen; why did she not say: I am divorced, I am divorced."
"11589","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: When she says to her husband, 'You are divorced,' it is one; they are alike — whether she says, 'I am divorced,' or, 'You are divorced.' From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, the same."
"11590","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Abu al-Sha'tha, asked about a man who granted his wife authority over her affair, and she said: I have accepted. He said: It is nothing; he has more right to her."
"11591","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: Her saying, 'I have accepted,' is nothing. Ibn Jurayj said: And Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz and Ibn Shihab, as I was told, used to say: 'I have accepted' is nothing, and my view follows that."
"11592","From al-Thawri, concerning a man who grants his wife authority, and she says: I have accepted that, he said: It is nothing."
"11593","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If he grants her authority and she says: I have accepted, it is one divorce, and he has more right to her — unless he says after that: Your affair is in your hand, and she says: I have accepted, then it follows what he granted her. Then she says: I have accepted one. I said: If she says nothing, but stands moving her goods and leaves for her family, it is nothing."
"11594","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said to him, a man said: Your affair is in your hand, three times, and she accepted, he said: One. Amr said: Her saying, 'I have accepted,' is nothing."
"11595","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: If he gives her choice and she says: I have accepted myself, it is one divorce, and he has more right to her."
"11596","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, concerning the view of Ibn Mas'ud, he said: When he grants her authority over her affair and they part before she decrees anything, she has no authority."
"11597","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: When a man gives his wife choice and she does not choose in her sitting, it is nothing. From al-Thawri, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu al-Sha'tha, the same."
"11598","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: When he grants her authority over her affair and she says nothing until they part from their sitting, she has no say, and nothing is in her hand. If he retracts it before she says anything, until she rises from that sitting, she has no choice."
"11599","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed us that Abu al-Sha'tha used to say: When a man grants his wife authority over her affair, if they part from that sitting before she says anything, she has nothing. If he retracts his affair before she says anything, she has nothing."
"11600","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu al-Sha'tha, he said: When a man grants his wife authority, the word is what she says in her sitting; if they part without her saying anything, she has no authority. Amr said: Abu al-Sha'tha said: How can he walk among people while his wife's affair is in another's hand?"
"11601","Ibn Jurayj reported from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, who said: If a man gives his wife a choice and she says nothing until she stands up, then it is nothing."
"11602","From al-Thawri, concerning a man who gives his wife control of her affair and then takes it back before she stands up: he said, He has no right to take back what has left his hands."
"11603","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Salim, from al-Shabi, who said: She has the choice so long as she remains in her sitting."
"11604","From al-Muthanna ibn al-Sabbah, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from Abdullah ibn Amr, that Umar ibn al-Khattab and Uthman ibn Affan used to say: If a man gives his wife a choice or gives her control, and they part from that sitting without deciding anything, then her affair returns to her husband."
"11605","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, who said: If she remains silent, that is her consent; and another besides him mentioned from Ibrahim, who said: She has the choice while she is in her sitting, and if she does not choose in her sitting, then it is nothing."
"11606","From Mamar, from a man, from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim, concerning a woman whose husband gives her a choice and she says nothing until they part from that sitting: he said, She has no choice except in that sitting."
"11607","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Said, from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim, who said: She may choose so long as she has not moved from her seat, and if she moves, she has no choice."
"11608","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from al-Hakam, from Ali, who said: It is in her hand until she speaks."
"11609","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatada, both of whom said: Her affair is in her hand until she decides; Qatada said: But if her husband has intercourse with her before she decides."
"11610","From Mamar, from Amr, from al-Hasan, who said: Her affair is in her hand in that sitting and in other than it, until she decides therein."
"11611","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, who said: I asked al-Shabi about a man who put his wife's affair in the hand of a man, and he divorced her three times? Umar said: One divorce, and he has no return to her; and Ali said: Whoever held the knot and then put it in the hand of another, it is as it ran upon his tongue."
"11612","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Shihab informed me that he heard al-Harith ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Rabia say: If he puts his wife's affair in the hand of her guardian and he divorces three times, she has become separated from him."
"11613","From Ibn Uyayna, from Yahya ibn Said, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, that Aisha married al-Mundhir to the daughter of Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr while he was not present, so Abd al-Rahman came and said: O servants of Allah, are my daughters to be disposed of without me? So Aisha ordered al-Mundhir to put the matter in his hand, and he returned it to him, and that matter was not counted as anything."
"11614","Ibn Jurayj reported: I said to Ata: Can she transfer it to another? He said: No. I said: Did Aisha not transfer Hafsa when al-Mundhir gave her control of her affair? He said: No, she only presented to her whether to divorce her or not, and she did not give her control of her affair."
"11615","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Tawus informed me, and I said to him: What did your father say about a man who gives control of his wife's affair to a man: does the man own the right to divorce her? He said: No."
"11616","From al-Thawri, who said: If a man says to a man, Go and divorce my wife three times, and he divorces her once, it is valid, because one is part of three; but if he says, Divorce once, and he divorces three times, then it is a contradiction and is nothing."
"11617","From Mamar, who said: If he says, Divorce her three times, and he divorces her once, he said: It is one."
"11618","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Qatada, concerning a man who gave control of his wife's affair to a man: they both said, It is in his hand until he decides concerning it."
"11619","From al-Thawri, who said: If a man says to another, My wife's affair is in your hand, he has no right to take it back unless the man returns it to him."
"11620","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: A man says to his wife, Your affair is in your hand after a day or two days: he said, This is nothing. I said: Then he sends a man that her affair is in her hand for a day or an hour: he said, I do not know, I do not think this is anything; and I say: Aisha had sent the transfer of Abd al-Rahman near to them, and I had heard him before this say: It is in her hand."
"11621","From Mamar, from Qatada, concerning a man who says to his wife, Your affair is in your hand after two days: he said, Her affair is in her hand until she utters that."
"11622","From Hisham, from al-Hasan, concerning a man who gives his wife control of her affair until a term: he said, It is in her hand so long as he does not have intercourse with her."
"11623","From Mamar, from Qatada, concerning a man who says to his wife, Your affair is in your hand until the end of ten days: he said, It is in her hand unless he has intercourse with her, and it stands upon what she said."
"11624","From al-Thawri, concerning a man who gives his wife control of her affair until a term: he said, It is until the term, and likewise if he says to his slave, You are free in a year, then it is until the term; this is the saying of Ibrahim and others."
"11625","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who put his wife's affair in the hand of two men, and one of them divorced and the other returned: he said, She is divorced."
"11626","From Mamar, from Qatada, concerning a man who put his wife's affair in the hand of two men, and one of them divorced three times and the other returned: he said, She is divorced three times."
"11627","From al-Thawri, concerning a man who gave his wife's affair to several different people and some of them divorced: he said, None of them may divorce without the other."
"11628","From Mamar, from Qatada, concerning a man who put his wife's affair in two hands: he said, If one of them dies before she decides anything, neither inherits from the other; and if he put her affair in the hand of another than her, and the one in whose hand he put her affair dies before deciding anything, then she is not lawful for him until she marries another husband; and if one of them dies before deciding anything, they do not inherit from each other. Mamar said: And I heard one who says, If the one in whose hand he put her affair dies before deciding anything, then it is nothing, and it is more pleasing to me than the saying of Qatada."
"11629","From Mamar, who said: I asked Amr about a man who put his wife's affair in the hand of a man, and the man died before deciding anything? He said: If he wishes, he divorces her once and takes her back."
"11630","From Mamar, from Qatada, who said: If a man says to his wife, If you do such and such, then your affair is in your hand: he said, Then if she does it, her affair is in her hand."
"11631","From Mamar, from Qatada, concerning a man who married a woman and stipulated upon her: If you do such and such, then her affair is in her hand: he said, Every condition before marriage is nothing, and every condition after marriage is binding upon him."
"11632","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: What if he mistreats her, and is not just to her in division, and he is in a land and abandons spending upon her? Then he said: If I return to that, then her affair is in her hand: he said, This is nothing, though I had heard him before this say: It is in her hand."
"11633","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: Giving control and giving choice are the same. Then I mentioned that to Ayyub, and he said: I do not see them but the same."
"11634","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: Giving control and giving choice are the same."
"11635","From al-Thawri, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, who said: Giving control and giving choice are the same. From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Shabi, the like of that; from al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Shabi, who said: It is in the saying of Ali, Umar, and Zayd ibn Thabit the same."
"11636","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: If a man gives his wife a choice and she chooses him, then it is nothing; but if she chooses divorce, then it is one, and he has more right to her. And it reached us from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz the like of the saying of Ata."
"11637","From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Ibn Masud, who said: If she chooses her husband, then it is nothing, and if she chooses herself, then it is one, and he has more right to her."
"11638","From Mamar, from Qatada, that Ali said: If he gives her a choice and she chooses him, then it is one, and he has more control over her; and if she chooses herself, then it is one, and she has more right to herself; and Qatada used to give fatwa by it."
"11639","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, concerning a man who gives his wife a choice: he said, If she chooses herself, then it is one irrevocable; and if she chooses her husband, then it is one, and he has more right to her. He said: And Umar ibn al-Khattab and Abdullah ibn Masud said: If she chooses herself, then it is one and it is one; and if she chooses her husband, then nothing. He said: And Zayd ibn Thabit said: If she chooses herself, then it is three."
"11640","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Dhakwan, who said: Kharija ibn Zayd ibn Thabit and Aban ibn Uthman narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Thabit, who said: If a man gives his wife control of her affair and she chooses herself, then it is one, and he has more right to her."
"11641","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Shabi, that Ali said: If she chooses herself, then it is one irrevocable; and if she chooses her husband, then it is a divorce with return to her."
"11642","And Zayd ibn Thabit said: If she chooses herself, then it is three."
"11643","And Umar and Abdullah ibn Masud said: If she chooses her husband, then no harm; and if she chooses herself, then it is one, and he has return to her."
"11644","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan say: If he gives her a choice and she chooses her husband, then it is one, and he has return to her."
"11645","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan say: If he gives her a choice and she chooses her husband, then it is one: al-Hasan raised it to Zayd ibn Thabit, and al-Hasan used to give fatwa by it, and say: He has more control over her; and if she chooses herself, then it is three: al-Hasan raised it to Zayd ibn Thabit, and al-Hasan used to give fatwa by it until he died."
"11646","From Mamar, who said: It reached me that a man said to a man, Give your wife a choice, and you shall have a camel; so he gave her a choice and she chose her husband. Then he said: Give her a choice, and you shall have a camel; so he gave her a choice and she chose her husband. Then he said: Give her a choice again, and you shall have a camel; so he gave her a choice and she chose her husband. Then the man who had asked him to give his wife a choice said: She has become forbidden to you. Then he came to Ali, who said: Do not approach her, or I shall stone you."
"11647","From al-Thawri, who said: Mukhawwal narrated to me, from Abu Jafar Muhammad ibn Ali, who said: Ali ibn Abi Talib said, concerning a man who gives his wife a choice: If she chooses her husband, then nothing; and if she chooses herself, then it is one irrevocable. Mukhawwal said: But he is narrated from with other than this, so he said: It is only something they found in the sheets. Al-Thawri said: And this saying is the most just of sayings to me and the most beloved to me."
"11648","From Mamar, from Asim, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, who said: I would not mind giving my wife a choice a hundred times, each time she would choose me. From Ibn Uyayna, from Ismail, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, the like of it."
"11649","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: Aisha said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave us a choice, and we chose Allah and His Messenger, and he did not count that as a divorce. Mamar said: And one who heard al-Hasan informed me, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only gave them a choice between this world and the Hereafter, and did not give them a choice in divorce."
"11650","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ismail, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, from Aisha, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave his wives a choice; was that a divorce?"
"11651","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, who said: I heard Makhul say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave his wives a choice, and they chose him, and that was not a divorce. He said: So Makhul used to say: If a man gives his wife a choice and she chooses him, then it is nothing; and if she chooses herself, then it is one, and he has more right to her."
"11652","Ibn Jurayj reported: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdillah say, concerning a man who gives his wife a choice and she chooses divorce: he said, It is one, and I dislike giving her a choice."
"11653","From Ibn Uyayna, who said: Abu al-Zinad informed me, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Zayd ibn Thabit, concerning a man who gave his wife control of her affair, and she divorced herself three times: he said, It is one."
"11654","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, from Ibn Masud, and he was asked about a man who said to his wife, Choose, and she was silent; then he said, Choose, and she was silent; then he said to her a third time, Choose, and she said, I have chosen myself: he said, It is three."
"11655","From al-Thawri, from Bayan, from al-Shabi, who said: If he gives her a choice three times and she chooses herself, then she has become separated from him; and if he gives her a choice once and she chooses herself three times, then it is one."
"11656","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: If he says, Choose, then Choose, then Choose, and she says, I have chosen myself, then I have chosen myself, then I have chosen myself: he said, Then it is only one. He said: But if he said, Choose, and she said, I have chosen myself; then he said, Choose, and she said, I have chosen myself; then he said, Choose, and she said, I have chosen myself, each time in one sitting, they would be three. I said to Ata: Then I said, You are divorced, and I am divorced: he said, It is one."
"11657","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: If a man says to his wife, Choose, and she says, I have chosen myself; then he says, Choose, and she says, I have chosen myself; then he says, Choose, and she says, I have chosen myself, then she has gone from him."
"11658","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, who said: Muhammad ibn Abi Atiq gave his wife a choice, and she divorced herself three times, so Muhammad asked Zayd ibn Thabit, and he made it one, and he has more control over her. Then I narrated this hadith to Ayyub, and he said: The like of this has reached me from Zayd, and I heard in that sitting a man of the people of Madina narrating, from a man of the people of Madina, from Zayd ibn Thabit, the like of the saying of Ayyub, from Zayd ibn Thabit."
"11659","From al-Thawri, concerning a man who gives his wife a choice three times: he said, If she chooses herself, then it is three times; and if she chooses her husband, then nothing; and if he gives her a choice once and she chooses herself, then it is one, and she has more right to herself, and he may propose to her if he wishes."
"11660","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, who said: Al-Shabi was asked about a man who gave his wife a choice and she was silent, then he gave her a choice a second time and she was silent, then he gave her a choice a third time and she chose herself: he said, She is not lawful for him until she marries another husband."
"11661","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Dhakwan, who said: Kharija ibn Zayd and Aban ibn Uthman ibn Affan narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Thabit, who said: If a man gives his wife control of her affair and she chooses herself, then it is one, and he has more control over her."
"11662","From Ibn Uyayna, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Zayd ibn Thabit, concerning a man who put his wife's affair in her hand, and she divorced herself three times: he said, It is one."
"11663","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: If he says, Choose if you wish, and she wishes to choose, then she has the choice; but if she says nothing until they part from that sitting of theirs, then she has no choice once they part."
"11664","From Mamar, from Qatada, who said: If he says, Choose if you wish, and she says, I have chosen myself, then it is one, and she has more control over herself."
"11665","From al-Thawri, from Ashath, from al-Hasan, who said: If he says, You are divorced if you wish, then she is in the position of choice so long as they remain in the sitting."
"11666","From al-Thawri, who said: If he says, You are divorced if you wish, then the choice is hers so long as she remains in her sitting; but if she decides nothing in that sitting, then she has no will after that. And if he says, You are divorced whenever you wish, and when you wish, then whenever she wishes, and when she wishes, one divorce, she has nothing beyond that. And if he says, You are divorced whenever you wish, then she is divorced whenever she wishes, until she becomes separated by three, and it is hers even if it falls upon her. And if he says, You are divorced as many as you wish, then she is divorced in that sitting as many as she wishes: if she wishes three, and if she wishes one; and if she stands from that sitting before saying anything, then she has no will."
"11667","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: If a man says to his wife, You are divorced if you wish, and she says, I have wished, then she is divorced."
"11668","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: If he says, You are divorced if you wish, and she wishes, then she is divorced."
"11669","From Mamar, from Qatada, who said: If a man says to his wife, You are divorced if you wish: he said, If she says, I have wished, she is divorced once; and if she says, I have not wished, then it is nothing."
"11670","From Mamar, from Qatada, who said: If he says to his wife, If you wish I shall divorce you, and she says, I have wished, and the husband says, I shall not do, then it is nothing."
"11671","From al-Thawri, who said: If a man gives his wife a choice while he is sick, and she chooses herself, or she ransoms herself, or she asks him for divorce, then there is no inheritance between them, because that is from her side."
"11672","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: If he divorces her and in her belly are twins, and he does not take her back until she delivers one, while in her belly is the other, then she is his wife so long as she has not delivered all her burden."
"11673","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata al-Khurasani informed me, from Ibn Abbas, who said: If he divorces her and in her belly are twins, and she delivers one of them, her husband may take her back so long as she has not delivered the other."
"11674","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: He has return to her until she delivers all her burden, when he has not made her divorce final."
"11675","From Mamar, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, who said: He has return to her so long as she has not delivered all her burden, when there were two in her belly."
"11676","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Salim, from al-Shabi, who said: He has return to her until she delivers the other, when he has not made her divorce final."
"11677","From Mamar, from Qatada, from Ibn al-Musayyib, al-Hasan, and Sulayman ibn Yasar, who said: He has return to her until she delivers the other of them, when he has not made her divorce final. Qatada said: And Ikrima said: If she delivers one, her waiting period has ended."
"11678","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: Whatever divorced woman, or woman whose husband died, finds in her belly like dry dung, not knowing whether in her belly is a child or not, while she feels like movement, doubting: he said, Then she should not hasten to marry until it becomes clear that there is no child in her belly."
"11679","From Mamar, and he was asked about her, and he said: I have not heard concerning it anything, that Umar set for the one who doubts to wait nine months, then observe a waiting period of three months."
"11680","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: The finally separated pregnant woman is not his in anything, except that he spends upon her because of his child; but if she is not pregnant, then she has no maintenance."
"11681","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning the finally separated pregnant woman: he said, She has maintenance until she delivers her burden."
"11682","From Mamar, from Qatada, who said: She has maintenance until she delivers her burden, and they do not inherit from each other."
"11683","From Ibn Jurayj, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, who said: There is no maintenance for the finally separated woman unless she is pregnant."
"11684","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Hisham ibn Urwa narrated to me that he asked him about a man who divorces his wife finally: does one of them inherit from the other, and does she have maintenance? He said: Neither inherits from the other, and she has no maintenance unless she is pregnant."
"11685","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, concerning the divorced pregnant woman: he said, She has maintenance and no lodging; he said: And Hammad said: She has maintenance and lodging."
"11686","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata informed me, who said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Asim ibn Thabit informed me, that Fatima bint Qays, sister of al-Dahhak ibn Qays, informed him, and she was with a man of Banu Makhzum, and she informed him that he divorced her three times, and he went out to some of the campaigns, and he ordered an agent of his to give her some maintenance, so she deemed it little. Then she went to one of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered while she was with her, and she said: O Messenger of Allah, this is Fatima bint Qays, so-and-so divorced her, and he sent her some maintenance, and she returned it, claiming it is something given as a favour. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He spoke the truth. Then he said to her: Move to Umm Maktum and observe your waiting period with her. Then he said: Except that Umm Maktum is a woman whose visitors are many; rather move to Abdullah ibn Umm Maktum, for he is blind. So she moved to him until her waiting period ended. Then Abu Jahm and Muawiya ibn Abi Sufyan proposed to her, so she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking his counsel about them, and he said: As for Abu Jahm, I fear for you his beating with the stick; and as for Muawiya, he is a man poor of wealth. Then she married Usama ibn Zayd after that."
"11687","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, who said: Fatima bint Qays narrated to me that she was with Abu Amr ibn Hafs ibn al-Mughira, and he divorced her the last of three divorces, and she claimed that she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and sought his fatwa about leaving her house, so he ordered her, as she claimed, to move to Ibn Umm Maktum the blind; but Marwan refused except to suspect the hadith of Fatima about the leaving of the divorced woman from her house. From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Shihab informed me, from Urwa, that Aisha denied that upon Fatima."
"11688","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: Ubaydullah ibn Abdillah ibn Utba informed me that Abu Amr ibn Hafs ibn al-Mughira went out with Ali to Yemen, and he sent to his wife Fatima bint Qays the remaining divorce that had remained of her divorce, and al-Harith ibn Hisham and Ayyash ibn Abi Rabia assigned maintenance for her, so she deemed it little. They said to her: By Allah, you have no maintenance unless you are pregnant. So she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned her matter to him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: You have no maintenance. And she sought his permission to move, and he permitted her. She said: Where, O Messenger of Allah? He said: To Ibn Umm Maktum, and he was blind; she would lay down her garments with him and he would not see her. When her waiting period passed, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married her to Usama ibn Zayd. Then Marwan sent Qabisa ibn Dhuayb to her asking her about that, and she narrated to him, and he came to Marwan and informed him. Marwan said: I have not heard this hadith except from a woman; we shall take the safeguard upon which we found the people. When the saying of Marwan reached Fatima, she said: Between me and you is the Quran; Allah the Mighty and Majestic said: Nor let them leave except that they commit a clear indecency, and those are the limits of Allah, and whoever transgresses the limits of Allah has wronged himself; you do not know, perhaps Allah will bring about after that a matter. She said: This is for one who has return; so what matter occurs after three? How do you say: She has no maintenance if she is not pregnant, then upon what do you confine her? Abd al-Razzaq said: And Mamar narrated to us this hadith first, then he narrated to us this other after."
"11689","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: Ubaydullah ibn Abdillah ibn Utba informed me that Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Uthman divorced, while he was a young lad in the governorship of Marwan, the daughter of Said ibn Zayd, whose mother was the daughter of Qays, and he divorced her finally. Her maternal aunt Fatima bint Qays sent to her and ordered her to move from the house of her husband Abdullah ibn Amr. Marwan heard that and sent to her and ordered her to return to her dwelling, and asked her what had prompted her to move before her waiting period ended. She sent informing him that Fatima bint Qays had given her fatwa with that, and had informed her that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had given her fatwa to leave, or he said: to move, when Abu Amr ibn Hafs al-Makhzumi divorced her. So Marwan sent Qabisa ibn Dhuayb to Fatima bint Qays asking her about that, and she informed him that she had been under Abu Amr ibn Hafs al-Makhzumi. She said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had put Ali in command over part of Yemen, so her husband went out with him, and he sent to her the remaining divorce that remained for her, and he ordered Ayyash ibn Abi Rabia and al-Harith ibn Hisham to spend upon her. They said: By Allah, she has no maintenance unless she is pregnant. She said: So I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him, and he said: You have no maintenance, unless you are pregnant. And she sought his permission to move, and he permitted her. She said: Where shall I move, O Messenger of Allah? He said: With Ibn Maktum, and he was blind; she would lay down her garments with him and he would not see her. She remained there until her waiting period passed, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married her to Usama ibn Zayd. Qabisa ibn Dhuayb returned to Marwan and informed him of that. Marwan said: I have not heard this hadith except from a woman, so we take the safeguard upon which we found the people. When that reached Fatima, she said: Between me and you is the Book of Allah the Mighty and Majestic; Allah the Exalted said: Then divorce them for their waiting period, until: you do not know, perhaps Allah will bring about after that a matter. What matter occurs after three? It is only the return of a man to his wife. How do you say: She has no maintenance if she is not pregnant, then how is a woman confined without maintenance?"
"11690","From Ibn Uyayna, from al-Mujalid, from al-Shabi, who said: Fatima bint Qays narrated to me, and she had been with Abu Hafs ibn Amr, or with Abu Amr ibn Hafs, and she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about maintenance and lodging, and she said: He said to me: Listen from me, O daughter of the family of Qays, and he gestured with his hand, stretching it over part of his face as though screening himself from her, as though saying to her: Be silent; maintenance is only for a woman upon her husband when he has return over her, and when he has no return over her, then she has no maintenance and no lodging. Go to so-and-so woman, or he said: Umm Sharik, and observe your waiting period with her. Then he said: No, she is a woman to whom people gather, or he said: at whose place conversation is held; observe your waiting period in the house of Ibn Umm Maktum."
"11691","From Salama ibn Kuhayl, from al-Shabi, from Fatima bint Qays, who said: My husband divorced me three times, so I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him, and he said: You have no maintenance and no lodging. He said: Then I mentioned that to Ibrahim, and he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: We do not leave the Book of our Lord and the Sunna of our Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); she has maintenance and lodging."
"11692","From al-Thawri, and we asked him about a woman who claims pregnancy? He said: Ibn Abi Layla used to send women to her to look at her, and if they recognized that and believed her, he gave her maintenance, and he took from her a guarantor."
"11693","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that Ibn Abbas said: The finally separated woman observes her waiting period wherever she wishes."
"11694","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, that Ali said, concerning the finally separated woman: She has no maintenance and no lodging."
"11695","Ibn Jurayj reported: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdillah say: The finally separated woman observes her waiting period wherever she wishes."
"11696","Ibn Jurayj reported: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdillah say: My maternal aunt was divorced and she wanted to cut her date-palms, so a man forbade her to go out, and she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Yes, cut your date-palms, for perhaps you may give charity, or do a good deed."
"11697","From Mamar, who said: One who heard al-Hasan and Ikrima informed me, both saying: The finally separated woman observes her waiting period as she wishes, that is, wherever she wishes."
"11698","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, who said: The divorced woman performs Hajj in her waiting period."
"11699","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus and Ata, both of whom said: The widow and the finally separated woman perform Hajj, and Umra, and move, and spend the night."
"11700","Ibn Jurayj and Mamar reported, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwa, from Aisha, that she used to forbid the divorced woman to leave her house until her waiting period ended."
"11701","Ibn Jurayj reported: Maymun ibn Mihran informed me, who said: I mentioned to Ibn al-Musayyib the hadith of Fatima: he said, Fatima has put the people to trial."
"11702","From Abdullah ibn Muharrar, from Maymun ibn Mihran, and Mamar from Jafar ibn Burqan, from Maymun ibn Mihran, who said: I asked Ibn al-Musayyib, Does the woman divorced three times leave her house? He said: No. I said: Then where is the hadith of Fatima? He said: She is a woman who put the people to trial; she was sharp-tongued against her in-laws."
"11703","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, who said: The finally separated woman does not move from her husband's house until her term is free."
"11704","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Alqama, that a man divorced his wife three times, and she refused to sit in her house, so he came to Ibn Masud, who said: She wishes to go out to her family. He said: Confine her and do not let her. He said: She refuses me. He said: Then fetter her. He said: She has brothers harsh of necks. He said: Then seek aid against them from the commander."
"11705","From Abdullah ibn Kathir, from Shuba, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Shurayh, concerning the woman divorced three times: he said, She has maintenance and lodging."
"11706","From Jafar ibn Sulayman, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, that when he divorced a woman of his women, he set her apart from his house until her waiting period ended, then she would move after."
"11707","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Hisham ibn Urwa narrated to me, from his father, that he asked him about a man who divorced his wife finally while he was sick? He said: Neither inherits from the other, and she has no maintenance unless she is pregnant, or he divorces harmfully in his sickness, and he dies while she is in her waiting period."
"11708","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who divorced his wife while she was a pilgrim: he said, She observes her waiting period in her journey."
"11709","From Mamar, from Qatada, who said: The woman who ransoms herself observes her waiting period wherever she wishes."
"11710","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: She observes in her house, and every divorced woman, and the woman of mutual cursing."
"11711","From Mamar, who said: I said to al-Zuhri, concerning a woman observing a waiting period from death or divorce, and she miscarries? He said: Her term has become free. He said: Even if it was a lump or a clot? He said: Yes; Mamar said it, and Qatada said it."
"11712","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: If a woman casts a distinct fall, then there is no way to her sale."
"11713","Ibn Jurayj reported, from Ata, who said: The widow observes four months and ten, even if her husband did not touch her, even if she is nursing or weaned. Mamar said: And one who heard al-Hasan informed me saying the like of it."
"11714","Ibn Jurayj reported, from Ata, who said: It does not harm the widow where she observes."
"11715","Ibn Jurayj reported: Ata informed me, from Ibn Abbas, who said: Allah only said: She observes four months and ten, and He did not say: She observes in her house; she counts wherever she wishes. From al-Thawri, from a man, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, the like of it."
"11716","Ibn Jurayj reported: Ata informed me that Aisha took her sister, the daughter of Abu Bakr, in her waiting period for Hajj or Umra, while Talha ibn Ubaydillah had been killed leaving her. Ibn Jurayj said: Then Ibn Shihab informed me, from Urwa, from Aisha, that she is Umm Kulthum."
"11717","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa, who said: Aisha took out her sister Umm Kulthum, when Talha ibn Ubaydillah had been killed leaving her, to Makka for an Umra. Urwa said: Aisha used to give fatwa to the widow to go out in her waiting period."
"11718","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, who said: Aisha took her sister for Hajj in her waiting period, and there was the tribulation and its fear. Al-Thawri said: Then Ubaydullah ibn Umar informed me that he heard al-Qasim ibn Muhammad say: The people disapproved that upon her."
"11719","From al-Thawri, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Shabi, who said: Ali used to move them, saying: He would transfer them."
"11720","From Mamar, from Ayyub, or another, that Ali moved his daughter Umm Kulthum in her waiting period, when Umar had been killed leaving her."
"11721","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, asked about a man who went out with his wife in the desert and he died? He said: She returns to her house and observes in it, unless when he went out he had resolved upon her divorce, then she observes in her desert."
"11722","Ibn Jurayj reported: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abdillah say: The widow observes wherever she wishes."
"11723","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus and Ata, both of whom said: The widow performs Hajj, and Umra, and moves, and spends the night."
"11724","Ibn Jurayj reported: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from Salim ibn Abdillah ibn Umar, from Abdullah, that he used to say: It is not right that she spend a single night, when she is in a waiting period of death or divorce, saying: except in her house."
"11725","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, that Ibn Umar said: The widow does not leave in her waiting period from her husband's house."
"11726","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, who said: The widow does not leave from her husband's house."
"11727","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, and Mamar from Ayyub, from Nafi, who said: The daughter of Abdullah ibn Umar was observing a waiting period from the death of her husband, and she used to come to them with fire and converse with them; then when it was night, he ordered her to return to her house."
"11728","From Mamar, from Ayyub, that Umar ibn al-Khattab did not permit the widow to spend the night with her father except one night while he was dying."
"11729","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I heard Yahya ibn Said narrate that Umar ibn al-Khattab permitted the widow to spend the night with her father while he was sick, one night. Yahya said: So we hold that she may spend her whole day until night outside her house if she wishes, and return; and he mentioned women who did that by day in the time of Umar and others."
"11730","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Yusuf ibn Mahak, from his mother Musayka, that a widow visited her family in her waiting period and labour seized her, so they came to Uthman and asked him? He said: Carry her to her house while she is in labour."
"11731","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Alqama, who said: Ibn Masud was asked by women of Hamdan whose husbands had been announced dead to them, and they said: We feel lonely. Abdullah said: Gather by day, then each woman of you return to her house by night."
"11732","From Mamar, from Mansur, from Alqama, from Ibn Masud the like of it, except that he said: Their husbands had died of them in a plague that was in Kufa."
"11733","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from a man of Aslam, from Umm Salama, that a woman asked her whose husband had died leaving her, and she said: My father is sick. She said: Be in your house one of the two ends of the day."
"11734","Ibn Jurayj reported: Humayd al-Araj informed me, from Mujahid, who said: Umar and Uthman used to return them as pilgrims and performers of Umra from al-Juhfa and Dhu al-Hulayfa."
"11735","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Ibn al-Musayyib, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab returned women pilgrims or performers of Umra whose husbands had died, from the back of Kufa."
"11736","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn of Kab ibn Ujra, who said: My paternal aunt narrated to me, and she had been under Abu Said al-Khudri, that Furaya narrated to her that her husband went out seeking runaway slaves, until when he was at the edge of al-Qadum, and it is a mountain, they overtook them and killed him. He said: So she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned to him that her husband had been killed, and that he had left her in a dwelling not his, and she sought his permission to move, and he permitted her. She set out until when she was at the door of the chamber, he ordered her and she was returned, and he ordered her to repeat to him her story, and she did, and he ordered her not to leave until the decree reaches its term."
"11737","From Mamar, from Sad ibn Ishaq ibn Kab ibn Ujra, narrating from his paternal aunt Zaynab bint Kab, from Furaya, with this hadith: he said, So when it was the time of Uthman, a woman came to him asking him about that. Furaya said: So I was mentioned to him, and he sent to me and asked me, and I informed him. Then he ordered her not to leave from her husband's house until the decree reaches its term."
"11738","From al-Thawri, from Sad ibn Ishaq ibn Kab ibn Ujra, from Furaya bint Malik, that her husband was killed at al-Qadum. She said: So I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and she said that she had family. Then he ordered her to move; but when she turned, he returned her, and said: Remain in your house until the decree reaches its term, four months and ten."
"11739","Ibn Jurayj reported: Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr informed me that Sad ibn Ishaq ibn Kab ibn Ujra informed him, from his paternal aunt Zaynab bint Kab ibn Ujra, that Furaya, daughter of Malik, sister of Abu Said al-Khudri, informed her that a husband of hers went out, until when he was at Madina six miles away, at the edge of a mountain called al-Qadum, robbers rushed upon him and killed him. Furaya had been among Banu al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj in a dwelling that had not been her husband's, it was only a lodging. Her brothers came to her, among them Abu Said al-Khudri, and said: We have no means in our hands to give you and keep you, and nothing sets us right except to be together, and we fear loneliness upon you; so ask the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So she came and related to him what her brothers had said, and the loneliness, and she sought his permission to observe with them. He said: Do if you wish. She turned until when she was in the chamber, he said: Come, repeat what you said. She said: Then he said: Remain in your dwelling until the decree reaches its term. Then Uthman: a woman of his people sent to him asking him about moving from her husband's house to observe in another than it. He said: Do. Then he said to those around him: Has there come from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or from a companion, concerning the like of this anything? They said: Furaya narrates from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he sent to her, and she informed him, and he stopped at her saying, and he ordered the woman not to leave her house. She informed that this woman who sent to Uthman was Umm Ayyub bint Maymun ibn Amir al-Hadrami, and that her husband was Imran ibn Talha ibn Ubaydillah."
"11740","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abdullah ibn Kathir, who said: Mujahid said: Men were martyred at Uhud leaving their women, and they were neighbours in a house, so they came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: We feel lonely, O Messenger of Allah, by night, so we spend the night with one of us, until when we rise in the morning we scatter to our houses? Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Converse with one of you as it appears to you, until when you wish sleep, then let each woman come to her house."
"11741","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, who said: The widow does not leave unless her family takes up a dwelling and she takes up with them."
"11742","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Hisham ibn Urwa informed me, from his father, that he was asked about the widow: does she move? He said: She does not move unless her family takes up a dwelling and she takes up with them."
"11743","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: Those who took license concerning the widow took the saying of Aisha, and the people of resolve and piety took the saying of Ibn Umar."
"11744","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: There is no maintenance for the pregnant widow except from her own wealth."
"11745","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, who said: There is no maintenance for the pregnant widow, and the inheritances have become due."
"11746","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Amr ibn Dinar: Ibn Abbas said: She has no maintenance."
"11747","Ibn Jurayj reported: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Musa ibn Badhan died leaving a pregnant wife. Ibn Abbas was asked about maintenance for her and said: She has no maintenance. Then Ibn al-Zubayr was approached and said: Spend on her. Then he said to her family: If you wish. And he narrated to us that Abdullah ibn al-Musayyab - or he said Ibn al-Sa'ib, I doubt - al-A'idhi met him: She has no maintenance. He said: Do not spend on her if you wish."
"11748","Ibn Jurayj reported: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, from Jabir ibn Abdullah: He said: The widow whose husband has died has no maintenance; the inheritance suffices her."
"11749","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdullah: He said: The widow whose husband has died has no maintenance; the inheritance suffices her. From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, the same."
"11750","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyab, concerning the pregnant widow: He said: She has no maintenance."
"11751","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from al-Hasan and Ikrimah: They both said concerning the widow: She has neither maintenance nor lodging."
"11752","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub: He said: Ibn Sirin sent to Abd al-Malik ibn Ya'la asking him about the widow who is pregnant, and that was because of the woman over whom they differed. So Abd al-Malik ibn Ya'la granted her no maintenance."
"11753","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar: He said concerning the widow who is pregnant: She has maintenance. Al-Zuhri said: I mentioned that to Qabisah ibn Dhu'ayb and he said: She has no maintenance; if I had no choice but to act, I would make it from the share of the one in her womb."
"11754","From Ibn Jurayj: He said: Ibn Shihab was asked about the pregnant widow: Upon whom is her maintenance? He said: Ibn Umar used to hold that her maintenance, whether pregnant or not, is from what her husband left. But the rulers refused that and ruled that she has no maintenance."
"11755","From al-Thawri, from Ash'ath, from al-Sha'bi: Ali and Ibn Mas'ud used to say: The maintenance of the pregnant woman is from the whole of the estate."
"11756","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Shurayh: He said: The maintenance of the pregnant widow and the nursing (cost) are from the whole of the estate."
"11757","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim: He said: Our companions used to say: If the property has abundance, then it - meaning the nursing (cost) - is from his (the child's) share."
"11758","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim: He said: If his share completes his nursing (cost), it is from his share; otherwise it is from the whole of the estate."
"11759","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from Abdullah ibn Ma'qil: He said: The nursing (cost) is from his (the child's) share."
"11760","From al-Thawri - and we asked him - about a woman who claims pregnancy: He said: Ibn Abi Layla used to send women to her to examine her; if they recognized that and believed her, he gave her maintenance and took a guarantor from her."
"11761","From Ibn Jurayj, from Yahya ibn Sa'id: He said: Ibn al-Musayyab was asked about a widow who is in rented lodging: Who pays the rent? He said: Her husband (i.e. from his estate); if not, then the ruler."
"11762","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a woman whose husband died while she is in rented lodging: He said: It is upon her husband's property; she is only detained in his right over her."
"11763","From al-Thawri, from one of the jurists: He used to say: The widow used to have maintenance and lodging for a year; then it was abrogated by: Those of you who die and leave wives, they shall wait by themselves for four months and ten days. And it was abrogated by: As for pregnant women, their term is until they deliver their burden. So if she is pregnant and delivers, her waiting period ends; and if she is not pregnant, she waits four months and ten days."
"11764","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Shaybani and Isma'il, from al-Sha'bi, concerning a woman who consumes her share of her husband's wealth after his death, not knowing of his death: He said: Whatever she consumed after his death is upon her, taken from her share. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Isma'il, from al-Sha'bi, the same."
"11765","From al-Thawri, from Hammad and Mansur, from Ibrahim: He said: It is hers for having confined herself for him (her husband). And al-Sha'bi's saying is dearer to Sufyan."
"11766","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and Ata al-Khurasani, from Ibn al-Musayyab: He said: The irrevocably divorced woman observes mourning (ihdad) just as the widow does: she touches no perfume, wears no dyed garment, does not apply kohl, wears no jewelry, does not dye (her hands/feet with henna), and does not wear saffron-dyed clothes."
"11767","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Musayyab: He said: The divorced woman and the widow are alike with respect to adornment."
"11768","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim: He used to dislike adornment for the divorced woman over whom he (the husband) has no right of return."
"11769","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: He said: She does not put on new jewelry, but if it was (already) on her she does not remove it; she touches no perfume; she combs with henna and katam; she oils with oil scented with basil; and the oil containing spices was disliked."
"11770","From Ma'mar: He said: Qatadah informed me that he heard al-Hasan say: The irrevocably divorced woman does not observe mourning: she wears what she wishes and oils as she wishes."
"11771","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: He said: The irrevocably divorced woman may adorn herself; she maintains herself, while the revocably divorced woman (does so) for her husband."
"11772","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: He said: Ibn Abbas used to command the widow to keep away from perfume. Ata said: She was forbidden perfume and adornment; beware of every dress about which, when seen on her, it is said: She has adorned herself. She wears no dyed (clothes) and no jewelry. And he claimed it reached him from Ibn Abbas: The widow's keeping away from perfume and adornment."
"11773","Ibn Jurayj reported: Ata said: The widow is forbidden perfume and adornment; she does not apply ithmid kohl, because it is adornment and contains musk, nor hudad, for it reportedly contains wars (a yellow dye); but (she may use) aloes (sabr) if she wishes."
"11774","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas: He used to command the widow to keep away from perfume and adornment. Ibn Jurayj said: And Ata did not consider silver to be among the jewelry that is disliked."
"11775","From Ma'mar, from Budayl al-Uqayli, from al-Hasan ibn Muslim, from Safiyyah bint Shaybah, from Umm Salamah: She said: The widow does not wear jewelry, does not dye (with henna), and does not perfume herself."
"11776","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi': Ibn Umar said: The widow does not spend the night away from her house; she does not perfume herself, does not dye (with henna), does not apply kohl, touches no perfume, and wears no dyed garment except a garment of asb (Yemeni cloth) which she wraps herself in. From al-Thawri, from Ubaydullah and Ibn Abi Layla, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, the same."
"11777","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: He said: Asb cloth and black are disliked for the widow; she wears no dyed clothes, wears no jewelry, and touches no perfume."
"11778","From al-Thawri, from Abu al-Miqdam: Ibn al-Musayyab said: The widow does not perform Hajj or Umrah, does not wear a close-fitting (ornate) dress, and does not apply kohl."
"11779","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: He said: If the widow had silver jewelry on her when her husband died, she need not remove it if she wishes; but if she was not wearing it when he died, she should not wear it, for then she intends adornment. And he disliked all gold, saying: It is adornment, and he dislikes it for the widow and for others."
"11780","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and Ata al-Khurasani, from Ibn Abbas - Abu Sa'id said: And I saw in another's book: Ibn al-Musayyab in place of Ibn Abbas -: He said: The widow touches no perfume, wears no dyed garment, does not apply kohl, wears no jewelry, does not dye (with henna), and does not wear saffron-dyed clothes."
"11781","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: He said: She combs with henna and katam, oils with oil scented with basil; the oil containing spices is disliked; and she touches no perfume."
"11782","From Ibn Jurayj: He said: Ata said: If she has a necessity for ithmid or other perfume, let her apply kohl with it and treat herself with it. He said: She combs with henna and katam, oils with raw oil, and with these Persian oils; but anything containing spices, no; and she does not touch perfume with her hand."
"11783","From Ibn Jurayj: He said: Musa ibn Uqbah informed me, from Nafi': A'ishah bint Muti', in her mourning, used to do the like of that over Asim ibn Umar."
"11784","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: He said: Costus (al-qust) and perfume-nails (al-azfar) are not perfume."
"11785","From Malik, from Nafi': Safiyyah bint Abi Ubayd complained of her eye while she was in mourning over Ibn Umar, and she did not apply kohl until her eyes almost became bleary with discharge."
"11786","Ibn Jurayj reported: Musa ibn Uqbah informed me, from Nafi': Safiyyah bint Abi Ubayd complained of her eyes while she was in mourning over Ibn Umar, until the pain in her eyes became severe; she did not apply ithmid kohl but smeared her eye with aloes (sabr)."
"11787","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Nafi': When Ibn Umar died, Safiyyah bint Abi Ubayd complained of her eyes and used to apply kohl of aloes (sabr)."
"11788","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah: She said: We were commanded not to wear dyed clothes in mourning except asb cloth; we were commanded not to mourn over a deceased - or she said: over a dead person - for more than three days except for a husband; and we were commanded not to touch perfume except at the end of purity (from menses): costus and perfume-nails (al-azfar)."
"11789","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Umm al-Hudhayl, from Umm Atiyyah: She said, concerning the widow: She wears no dyed garment and does not perfume herself except with a dab of costus and azfar at her purity."
"11790","From Malik, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Humayd ibn Nafi': Zaynab bint Abi Salamah informed him of these three hadiths: That she entered upon Umm Habibah bint Abi Sufyan, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when Abu Sufyan died. So Umm Habibah called for perfume containing yellow dye, khaluq or other, and oiled a slave-girl from it, then touched her cheeks with it, then said: By Allah, I have no need of perfume, except that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: It is not lawful for a woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day to mourn over a dead person for more than three days, except over a husband, four months and ten days."
"11791","He said: And Zaynab said: I entered upon Zaynab bint Jahsh, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when her brother died. So she called for perfume and touched some of it, then said: By Allah, I have no need of perfume, except that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say upon the pulpit: It is not lawful for a woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day to mourn over a dead person for more than three nights, except over a husband, four months and ten days."
"11792","Zaynab said: And I heard Umm Salamah bint Abi Umayyah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), say: A woman came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, my daughter's husband has died and she complains of her eye; shall I apply kohl to her? He said: No, twice or thrice, each time saying: No. Then he said: It is only four months and ten days; one of you in the past would throw a dung at the end of a year. Humayd said: I said to Zaynab: What is throwing the dung at the end of a year? She said: In the Jahiliyyah, when a woman's husband died, she would enter a small hut - it was said to Malik: What is a hifsh? He said: A small hut of palm branches - and wear her worst clothes, and touch no perfume or anything, until a year passed over her. Then she would be brought an animal, a donkey or a sheep or a bird, and she would break her mourning with it. I said to him: What is breaking with it? He said: She wipes with it; rarely did she break with anything but it died. He said: Then she would come out and be given a dung and throw it, then afterwards she could use whatever perfume she wished."
"11793","From Malik, from Nafi', from Safiyyah bint Abi Ubayd, from A'ishah - or from Hafsah -: She said: It is not lawful for a woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day to mourn over a dead person for more than three days, except over a husband."
"11794","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from A'ishah: She said: It is not lawful for a woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day to mourn over a deceased for more than three days, except over a husband."
"11795","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah - and Ibn Jurayj from Hisham ibn Urwah - from his father: He used to say: A woman does not mourn for more than three days except over her husband, for she mourns over him until her waiting period ends."
"11796","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from al-Jarrah, freedman of Umm Habibah, from Umm Habibah: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day - or he said: who believes in Allah and His Messenger - to mourn over a deceased for more than three days, except over her husband, for she mourns over him four months and ten days."
"11797","From Ma'mar - and Ibn Jurayj from Hisham ibn Urwah -: A widow asked Urwah, saying: I have only a veil dyed with baqqam (brazilwood); shall I wear it? He said: No. She said: I have nothing else. He said: Dye it black."
"11798","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: Umm Salamah was asked about ithmid for the widow. They said: She is used to it and she complains of her eyes. She said: No, even if her eyes were gouged out."
"11799","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: He said: If she has a necessity for ithmid or other perfume, let her apply kohl with it and treat herself with it."
"11800","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: He said: The widow does not apply kohl unless she complains of her eyes, in which case she is treated with medicine."
"11801","From Ma'mar, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Mujahid: He said: A widow asked him, saying: My mother is a perfumer; shall I sell perfume? He said: No harm upon you. When she turned away, he said: Nevertheless it is disliked for her to handle perfume."
"11802","From Ibn Jurayj: He said: I said to Ata: What if he died and there are furnishings in her house? He said: I like that she remove them. I said: She sets up a conveyance in the season with adornment, being adorned in it? He said: No. He said: Then it is said: Who are these? And it is said: So-and-so has adorned herself then."
"11803","From Ibn Jurayj: He said: I said to Ata: The widow adorns a slave-girl of hers whom she sends on an errand. He said: No harm in that; she was only forbidden adornment herself. And I asked him about sabiri (fine cloth)? He said: It is transparent, so he disliked it for all women."
"11804","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Sulayman ibn Mishar: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: Do not dress your women in qibati (fine linen), for if it is not transparent it outlines."
"11805","From Ibn Jurayj: He said: I said to Ata: And sabiri is not transparent? It is said: No harm in it; and she wears fine white clothes. We said to him: Marwi and Harawi (cloths)? He said: Then it is adornment."
"11806","From Ibn Jurayj: He said: I said to Ata: Her hair? He said: She does not braid it as long as she has not (finished and) put on her (ordinary) clothes."
"11807","Ibn Jurayj reported: I said to Ata: Silver - her husband dies while adornment is upon her, what she did not newly put on? He said: No (she need not remove it). I said: She was widowed while wearing two silver anklets, two armlets, two earrings, a necklace and rings, all silver. He said: She need not remove it if she wishes; that is not adornment. I said: Pearls? He said: Adornment. I said: If the silver rings have turquoise or ruby stones? He said: She need not remove it if she wishes. And if any of that contains gold, let her remove it if she wishes, unless it is a small ring. And he dislikes gold for her and for others."
"11808","From Ibn Jurayj: He said: A man said to Ata: Gold anklets under the clothes? He said: Adornment."
"11809","From Ibn Jurayj: He said: I said to Ata: The earring (khurs)? He said: She does not remove it. But if she had none of this on her when he died, she should not wear it, because then she intends adornment. He said: I said: A necklace or a head-cover? He said: No, unless it is a small thing."
"11810","From Ibn Jurayj: He said: Ata said: If she was widowed while a young girl who has reached (the age of) men, even if she has not menstruated, upon her is what is upon one who has menstruated regarding proposals, adornment and perfume. And if she is a small girl who has not reached (puberty), it does not harm her family to adorn her or perfume her, if they wish."
"11811","From al-Thawri: He said: Umm al-walad (a slave-mother) goes out, perfumes herself and dyes (with henna); she is not like the widow when her master dies."
"11812","Ibn Jurayj reported: I said to Ata: How does the suitor speak? He said: He alludes and does not disclose anything: I have a need, and rejoice, for you are, by Allah's praise, sought after. And she says: I hear what you say; do not repeat anything, and do not say: Perhaps that."
"11813","Ibn Jurayj reported: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah informed me, from Tawus: He said to him: The best you say when you mention and propose is to say: She is of nobility, and she is of beauty and comeliness."
"11814","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid: He said: He alludes to her during her Hajj, saying: By Allah, you are beautiful, women are indeed among my needs, and you are toward good, if Allah wills."
"11815","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, concerning: Except that you say a proper saying: He said: He says: You are beautiful, and you are toward good, and women are indeed among my needs."
"11816","From Mansur, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas: He said: He says: I intend marriage."
"11817","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas: He said: He says: I intend marriage."
"11818","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid: He said: He says: You are beautiful, you are fair, you are sought after, you are toward good, and the like of this. From Ibn Jurayj, from Mujahid, the same."
"11819","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah: He said: He alludes to her: I am indeed desirous of you, you are beautiful, and women are indeed among my needs."
"11820","From al-Thawri, concerning His saying: Except that you say a proper saying: He said: He says: I am indeed desirous of you, and I hope, if Allah wills, that we come together."
"11821","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ikrimah: That a man may promise the woman's guardian without her knowledge."
"11822","Ibn Jurayj reported: I said to Ata: What if she pledged, contracted and promised a man during her waiting period to marry him, then it was completed for him; are they separated? He said: No. Ibn Jurayj said: And it reached me that Ibn Abbas said: It is better for him to leave her."
"11823","Ibn Jurayj reported: I said to Ata: The irrevocably divorced woman pledges a man and agrees with him during her waiting period? He said: Why should she pledge? He said: She says: I did not promise you."
"11824","From Ma'mar, concerning the irrevocably divorced woman: He said: She makes appointments during her waiting period without a covenant? He said: That is disliked."
"11825","From al-Thawri: He said: The irrevocably divorced woman and the widow are alike regarding promising (in waiting period)."
"11826","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, concerning His saying: Do not promise them secretly: He said: It is one who takes upon her a covenant or pledge that she confine herself and marry none but him."
"11827","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, concerning His saying: Do not promise them secretly: He said: He says: You are indeed among my needs."
"11828","From al-Thawri, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Muslim al-Batin, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, concerning His saying: Do not promise them secretly: He said: He does not bargain with her over such-and-such on condition she not marry another. Al-Sha'bi said: From Ibrahim al-Nakha'i it is said: It is fornication."
"11829","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, concerning His saying: Do not promise them secretly: He said: It is immorality."
"11830","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Mijlaz: He said: It is fornication."
"11831","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid: He used to dislike one saying: Do not give yourself to another before me."
"11832","From al-Thawri, from a man, from al-Dahhak, from Muzahim, concerning His saying: Or you conceal: Then he said: You hide."
"11833","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, concerning His saying: Until the prescribed term is reached: He said: Until the waiting period ends."
"11834","Ibn Jurayj reported: I said to Ata: What is: Mothers shall nurse their children two complete years? He said: If a woman wants to shorten from two years, it is a duty upon his mother to complete it for him; and she does not exceed them unless she wishes. And she is the divorced woman and the widow. And it is narrated that it is among people after they differed about the time of nursing."
"11835","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah: He said: If he (the father) and the mother want to wean their child before two years, and that is by mutual consent and consultation of them both, there is no harm."
"11836","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from Mujahid, concerning His saying: If they both desire weaning by mutual consent and consultation: He said: They consult about (weaning) before two years; she may not wean except with his permission, and he may not wean except with her permission."
"11837","From Ibn Jurayj: He said: I said to Ata: What is: No mother shall be harmed through her child, nor a father through his child? He said: She does not leave him with him by way of harm, nor does he withhold him from her for what he finds (of means)."
"11838","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, concerning His saying: No mother shall be harmed through her child: She casts him upon his father by way of harm; nor a father through his child: He says: Nor the father, who snatches him from her by way of harm, when she is content with the nursing wage that another would accept; she has more right to him when she is content with that."
"11839","From al-Thawri, from ...: He said: No mother shall be harmed through her child: He said: She casts away her child and does not nurse him; nor a father: He says: Nor the father, who snatches him from her. And upon the heir the like of that: He says: Upon the child's heir is the like of what is upon the father: he does not snatch him from her, and upon him is the remainder of nursing."
"11840","From Ibn Jurayj: He said: I said to Ata: What is: And upon the heir the like of that? He said: The heir of the child is like what was mentioned."
"11841","Ibn Jurayj reported: I said to Ata: Is the child's heir detained if the child has no property, for the wage of a wet-nurse, even if the heir dislikes it? He said: Would you leave him to die?"
"11842","From Ibn Jurayj: He said: Amr ibn Shu'ayb informed me that Ibn al-Musayyab informed him that Umar ibn al-Khattab bound the cousins of an infant cousin, a kalalah (without direct heirs), with maintenance upon him like the blood-money group. They said: He has no property. He said: So he bound them with maintenance upon him like the (sharing of) blood-money."
"11843","From al-Thawri, from Layth, from a man, from Ibn al-Musayyab: He informed him that Umar compelled a man to (pay for) the nursing of his brother's son."
"11844","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah and others, concerning His saying: And upon the heir the like of that: He said: It is upon the child's heir when the child has no property."
"11845","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: Umar ibn al-Khattab fined three men, each of whom inherits the child, the wage of his nursing."
"11846","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin: Abdullah ibn Utbah made a child's maintenance from his property, and said to his heir: Know that if he had no property we would have taken you for his maintenance. Do you not see that He says: And upon the heir the like of that."
"11847","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: He said: The nursing wage of a child whose father has died is in the child's share of the property. Ibn Jurayj said: Ibn Kathir said: Before the completion of two years."
"11848","From Ibn Jurayj: He said: I said to Ata: Did you hear anything definite about: Their provision and clothing? He said: No. And Ibn Kathir said: So give them their wages: Their provision and clothing."
"11849","Ibn Jurayj reported: I said to Ata: And if you wish to seek nursing for your children? He said: His mother and others, when you hand over what you give, you pay."
"11850","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim: He said: If its wage stands (is settled), his mother has more right to it. Sufyan said: If his mother refuses, a nurse is hired for him. If he has no property and they find no one to nurse him, Juwaybir informed me, from al-Dahhak, that he said: His mother is compelled to nurse him. But if they find someone to nurse him, the mother is not compelled."
"11851","From al-Thawri - and I asked him - about a man who dies leaving his wife nursing, while she has no property, and his mother refuses to nurse him: He said: She is not compelled to nurse him; it is upon the male relatives (asabah). He said: And dearer to me is that it be upon men and women, and upon his mother according to her share of inheritance from him."
"11852","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyab: Uthman ibn Affan gave the wife of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf inheritance after the waiting period ended, though he had divorced her while ill."
"11853","Ibn Jurayj reported: Ibn Abi Mulaykah informed us that he asked Ibn al-Zubayr about a man who divorces a woman irrevocably, then dies while she is in her waiting period. Ibn al-Zubayr said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf divorced the daughter of al-Asbagh al-Kalbi irrevocably, then died while she was in her waiting period, so Uthman gave her inheritance. Ibn al-Zubayr said: As for me, I do not hold that the irrevocably divorced woman inherits. Ibn Abi Mulaykah said: And she is the one who claims he divorced her while ill."
"11854","From Ibn Jurayj: He said: Ibn Shihab informed me - and I asked him - about a man who divorced his wife thrice in illness: How does she observe waiting if he dies, and does she inherit him? He said: Uthman ruled concerning the wife of Abd al-Rahman that she observes waiting and inherits him, and that he gave her inheritance after her waiting period ended, and that Abd al-Rahman's illness was prolonged. The name of al-Asbagh's daughter: Tumadir bint al-Asbagh ibn Ziyad ibn al-Husayn, and she is the mother of Abu Salamah."
"11855","From Ibn Jurayj: He said: Hisham ibn Urwah informed me that Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf divorced his wife while ill, then died, so Uthman gave her inheritance."
"11856","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman: Uthman gave the wife of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf inheritance after the waiting period ended, though he had divorced her while ill."
"11857","From Ibn Jurayj: He said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz informed him that Abd al-Rahman ibn Mukammil had three wives, one of them the daughter of Qariz. He said: Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman informed me that she was Juwayriyyah, and he was a man of great wealth. He went out trading until, at part of the way, paralysis seized him. People of Quraysh rode out to him, among them Nafi' ibn Tarif, and he divorced two of them. Then he remained after divorcing them two years, and they both inherited him; he died in Uthman's era, and I think it was he who gave them inheritance, and I do not think they remarried."
"11858","From Ibn Jurayj: He said: Ibn Shihab informed me that the wife of Ibn Mukammil was given inheritance by Uthman after her waiting period ended."
"11859","From Ibn Jurayj: He said: Ibn Shihab informed me that when it was ordered that Yazid ibn Abdullah be killed, he divorced his wife thrice, so she inherited him."
"11860","Ibn Jurayj reported: I said to Ata: A man divorces irrevocably while ill, then dies of that ailment. He said: She inherits him even if the waiting period ended, if he died in that illness and she did not remarry."
"11861","From Ma'mar, from one who heard al-Hasan: He says: She inherits him even if the waiting period ended, if he died of that illness. Al-Hasan said: They inherit one another, if he died of that illness. And other than al-Hasan said: She inherits him and he does not inherit her."
"11862","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: If he divorced her while ill, she inherits him as long as she is in the waiting period, and he does not inherit her."
"11863","From Ma'mar: He said: One who heard al-Hasan informed me, he says: They inherit one another if he died of that illness. Ma'mar said: And I heard one say: She inherits him and he does not inherit her."
"11864","From Ma'mar - and Ibn Jurayj from Hisham ibn Urwah - from his father: He said: If he divorced her irrevocably while ill and the waiting period ended, there is no inheritance between them."
"11865","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub and others, from Ibn Sirin: He said: If the waiting period ended, there is no inheritance between them."
"11866","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah: Shurayh said: If the waiting period ended, there is no inheritance between them."
"11867","From Ibn Jurayj: He said: I said to Ata: He divorced her irrevocably while ill, then recovered during her waiting period, then fell ill and died before her waiting period ended. He said: She has no inheritance, nor does he have return to her during her waiting period, nor does he inherit her if she died, in what his irrevocable divorce of her permits him; and it is not permitted against her regarding her inheritance."
"11868","From al-Thawri: He said: If he divorced his wife while ill, she is upon the longer of the two waiting periods: if four months and ten days is more than her menses, she mourns with the four and ten; and if menses is more, she mourns with the menses."
"11869","From Mughirah, from Ibrahim - and from Abu Sahl, from al-Sha'bi -: They both said: She observes waiting of four months and ten days."
"11870","From al-Thawri, concerning a man who divorced his wife twice while ill, then she menstruated twice, then he recovered and divorced her the third: He said: She does not inherit him, because he only separated her finally while healthy. But if he divorced her twice while healthy, then fell ill and made it final, she inherits him."
"11871","From Sufyan al-Thawri, concerning a man at death's approach who says: I divorced my wife ten years ago, and she has upon me a thousand dirhams. She says: You spoke truth. If what he acknowledged for her is more than her inheritance, she does not exceed the inheritance; and if it is less than the inheritance, she does not exceed it, because she accepted it."
"11872","From al-Thawri: He said: If the woman ransoms herself (khul') or he gives her choice and she chooses herself, or she asks him for divorce in his illness, she has no inheritance, because it came from her side."
"11873","From al-Thawri: He said: If a woman ransoms herself from her husband for ten thousand while ill, then dies, we make for him the measure of his inheritance from her. If his inheritance is less, we give him his inheritance; if his inheritance is more, he does not exceed the ten, because he accepted it. And if she recovers, it is permitted for him."
"11874","From al-Thawri, concerning a man whose wife says to him in her illness: I do not seek dower from my husband, then she dies: He said: Al-Sha'bi said: She is believed; Ibrahim and al-Hakam said: She is not believed."
"11875","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi: He said: If a woman absolves her husband of her dower while ill, it is not permitted. Muhammad ibn Yahya informed us of it from him."
"11876","From al-Thawri, concerning a woman whom her husband divorced thrice then died, and she says: He divorced me while ill, but his family says: Rather he divorced you while healthy - upon whom is the proof? He said: The word is hers, unless they bring proof that he divorced her while healthy."
"11877","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Umar: He said: Ghaylan ibn Salamah al-Thaqafi divorced his wives and divided his property among his sons - in Umar's caliphate. That reached Umar and he said: You divorced your wives and divided your property among your sons? He said: Yes. He said: By Allah, I see Satan, in what he steals of hearing, heard of your death and cast it into yourself; perhaps you will remain only a little. By Allah, if you do not take back your wives and return your property, I shall give them inheritance from you when you die; then I shall order your grave to be stoned as the grave of Abu Righal was stoned. Al-Zuhri said: And Abu Righal is the father of Thaqif. He said: So he took back his wives and returned his property. Nafi' said: He remained only seven (days) until he died."
"11878","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, concerning a man who divorces his wife before consummation while ill: He said: She has half the dower, no inheritance, and no waiting period upon her."
"11879","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Nakha'i and Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz: They both said: She has half the dower, no inheritance, and no waiting period upon her."
"11880","From Ibn Jurayj: He said: One whom I trust informed me that Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Adi concerning a man who divorced while ill before consummation, having fixed the dower: She has half of it. And he only took it from Sulayman ibn Yasar."
"11881","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Sha'bi: He said: There is no inheritance for the one not consummated with, if he divorced her while ill; she has half the dower. He said: And it reached me from Ibrahim al-Nakha'i the like of that. Abd al-Razzaq said: And the people are upon it, and I take it."
"11882","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan: He said: She has her full dower, she has inheritance, and upon her is the waiting period."
"11883","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: He said: She has only half the dower, and she has inheritance if he died of that ailment, as long as she did not remarry."
"11884","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah: Abu al-Sha'tha' said: She has the full dower, no inheritance, and no waiting period upon her."
"11885","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: He said: Every divorced woman has a provision (mut'ah), except one divorced before consummation with dower fixed: she has half the dower and no provision."
"11886","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: He said: Every divorced woman has provision, except one divorced before consummation with dower fixed: she has half the dower, no provision. From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, the same."
"11887","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata: He said: She has half the dower and no provision."
"11888","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyab: He said: She has half the dower and no provision."
"11889","From Abu Hanifah, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, concerning one who divorces his wife without consummation, having fixed for her: He said: She has half the dower and no provision."
"11890","From Abu Hanifah, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, concerning one who divorces his wife without consummation, having fixed for her: He said: She has half the dower and no provision. But if he divorced her before fixing, she has the provision and no dower."
"11891","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata, who said: If he did not consummate with her and did not fix a dower for her, she gets the muta gift and has no sadaq. From al-Thawri, from Abu Bistam, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, from Shurayh, the same, saying: She gets half."
"11892","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Ibrahim, about a woman for whom a dower was fixed but who was not consummated with: he said, She has nothing but half."
"11893","From al-Thawri, from Humayd al-Araj, from Mujahid, who said: Every divorced woman gets a provision, except one divorced before consummation: she gets half and has no provision."
"11894","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, who said: The divorced woman who was not consummated with gets a muta provision."
"11895","From al-Thawri, from al-Zubayr ibn Adi, from Zayd ibn al-Harith: Shurayh compelled a man, in the case of a divorced woman for whom her husband had fixed nothing, to give the provision."
"11896","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, who said: She is awarded half, from the dower of women like her."
"11897","Mamar from al-Zuhri, who said: Every divorced woman gets a muta provision."
"11898","From Mamar, from Ibn Shihab, who said: The provision is the same for the one consummated with and the one not consummated with; he says: They get the provision."
"11899","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, who said: Every divorced woman gets a provision; and he mentioned it from Abu Qilaba."
"11900","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan saying: Every divorced woman gets a provision."
"11901","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Shurayh, who said: I heard him saying to a man who had divorced: Give provision; I did not know what he answered him. Then I heard Shurayh saying: Do not refuse to be among the God-fearing; do not refuse to be among the doers of good."
"11902","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: There are two provisions: one enforced by the ruler, and the other a duty upon the God-fearing. Whoever divorces before fixing and consummating is held to the provision because no sadaq is due from him; whoever divorces after consummating and fixing, the provision is a duty upon him. From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, the same."
"11903","From al-Thawri, from Amr, from al-Hasan, who said: If he still holds the right of return, there is no provision due until the waiting period ends; if he does not hold the right of return, he gives provision at once."
"11904","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata, who said: Every woman who ransoms herself from her husband gets the provision. And he and Amr acted so. Ata said: If he gives her ownership of her affair and she divorces herself, or gives her choice and she chooses herself, or she ransoms herself from him by khul, or he divorces her conditionally upon doing or not doing something, then she gets the provision."
"11905","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, who said: The divorced woman and the woman who seeks khul get the provision. From al-Thawri, from Juwaybir, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, the same."
"11906","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ibn Shihab told me: The woman who seeks khul gets the matā, and the man is not compelled."
"11907","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: The woman who seeks khul gets a provision."
"11908","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: I know no fixed time for the provision; Allah the Mighty and Majestic said: Upon the well-off according to his means. And Ubaydullah ibn Adi gave a slave boy as provision."
"11909","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: It reached me that the one who divorces used to give a servant and a robe as provision. And Ibn Jurayj said from Ibn Shihab the same."
"11910","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Sad ibn Ibrahim: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf divorced his wife and gave her a servant as provision."
"11911","From al-Thawri and Ibn Jurayj, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, who said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf gave a black slave girl as provision. Ibn Jurayj said in his hadith: Its price was eighty dinars."
"11912","Ibn Jurayj reported from Musa ibn Uqba, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, who said: The least I see as sufficing for the provision of women is thirty dirhams or what resembles it."
"11913","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, who said: Provision used to be given as a servant, or maintenance and clothing; and al-Hasan ibn Ali gave wealth as provision, I think he said ten thousand, meaning dirhams."
"11914","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdillah, from his father, from al-Hasan ibn Sad, from his father, who said: Al-Hasan ibn Ali gave two wives twenty thousand and a skin of honey as provision; one of them, I see she was Jufiyya, said: A small provision from a beloved who parts."
"11915","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi: Shurayh gave five hundred dirhams as provision."
"11916","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim: Al-Aswad ibn Yazid gave three hundred dirhams as provision."
"11917","From Israil ibn Yunus, from Abu Ishaq: Shurayh gave five hundred dirhams as provision, al-Aswad gave three hundred dirhams as provision, and al-Hasan ibn Ali gave twenty thousand dirhams as provision; when it was brought and placed before her, she said: A small provision from a beloved who parts."
"11918","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Aban ibn Muawiya, who said: A man asked Ibn Umar: I am well-off, tell me my measure. He said: Give such, and clothe such. We calculated that and found it thirty dirhams."
"11919","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwa, who said: My father gave a servant as provision."
"11920","From Mamar, from Qatada, who said: The provision is a jilbab, a shirt, and a head-cover."
"11921","From al-Thawri, who said: The slave woman and the Christian woman, when divorced — [source text truncated/garbled at this point]."
"11922","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: Was offering made to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him? He said: A woman offered herself to him and he did not marry her; that belongs to none but the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. I said: What if one did so, marrying her — would that be without dower? He said: In what is established; and I say: Is there not in her marriage what you already know."
"11923","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abu al-Zubayr told me he heard Ikrima, freedman of Ibn Abbas, saying: Maymuna offered herself to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"11924","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatada: Maymuna bint al-Harith ibn Hazm offered herself to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"11925","Ibn Jurayj reported: Hisham ibn Urwa told me from Urwa that Khawla bint Hakim ibn al-Awqas of Bani Sulaym was among those who offered themselves to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him. From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, the same; he said: And I did not hear that he accepted her."
"11926","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: Gift-marriage is not lawful for anyone after the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; Allah the Exalted said: Exclusively for you apart from the believers."
"11927","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, who said: Gift-marriage is not lawful for anyone after the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"11928","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba: Ibn al-Musayyib and two men of knowledge with him said: Gift-marriage is not lawful for anyone after the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; even if he married her for a whip, she would be lawful."
"11929","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Yazid ibn Ubaydillah ibn Qusayt, who said: I was with Ibn al-Musayyib when he was asked about a man given good news of a slave girl, and one of the people said to him: Give her to me, so he gave her to him; Ibn al-Musayyib said to him: Gift is not lawful for anyone after the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him; even if he gave her a whip as dower, she would be lawful to him."
"11930","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi, who said: I heard him narrating that a woman came to the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and offered herself to him. He was silent, then she offered herself to him again and he was silent. He said: I saw her standing a long while offering herself to him while he was silent. A man stood — I think he was of the Ansar — and said: Messenger of Allah, if you have no need of her, marry her to me. He said: Have you anything? He said: No, by Allah, Messenger of Allah. He said: Go and seek something, even an iron ring. He went, then returned and said: By Allah, I found nothing except this garment of mine; split it between me and her. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: There is no surplus in your garment beyond you. Do you recite anything of the Quran? He said: Yes. He said: What? He said: Such-and-such sura and such-and-such sura. He said: I have given her to you in marriage for what you have of the Quran. He said: I saw him going away with her following him."
"11931","From Abu Hanifa, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, who said: If a woman offers herself to a man with witnesses and he consummates with her, she gets a dower like that of women of her standing; if he divorces her before consummating and fixing, she gets the provision."
"11932","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from one who heard Ali saying: Every divorce is valid except the divorce of the idiot. From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Amir ibn Rabia, from Ali, the same."
"11933","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri and Qatada, who said: Manumission and divorce are not valid from the foolish idiot gone in mind. From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, the same."
"11934","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata, who said: Divorce and manumission are not valid from a corrupt fool."
"11935","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Amir, from al-Shabi, who said: The divorce and marriage of the idiot are not valid."
"11936","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, who said: Whatever occurs in the lucid interval of the madman — divorce, manumission, or slander — is valid; whatever he does while insane is nothing."
"11937","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, who said: If the madman divorces and proof stands that he was sane, his divorce is valid; otherwise he is made to swear by Allah that he was not sane; if he swears, well, otherwise his divorce is valid. And he said about the madman deemed abominable who kills a man: he is made to swear by Allah that he was not sane; if he swears, the blood-money is imposed, otherwise he is killed."
"11938","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata, who said: The guardian of the one possessed by whisperings may divorce for him, but let him wait for him, perhaps he will recover."
"11939","From Mamar, from Qatada, about the idiot and the madman who cannot speak: he said, His guardian divorces for him."
"11940","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Amr ibn Shuayb, who said: We found in the book of Abdullah ibn Amr, from Umar ibn al-Khattab: When the one possessed keeps away from his wife, his guardian divorces for him. Sufyan said: We do not take this; we see it is an affliction that befell; if harm to her is feared, she is separated and maintained from his wealth."
"11941","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: His guardian does not divorce for him; let her be patient."
"11942","From Mamar, from al-Amash, from Abu Dhabyan, from Ibn Abbas: An insane woman committed indecency in the time of Umar, and Umar ordered her stoning. She was passed by Ali while the boys were saying: The mad woman of Banu Fulan is to be stoned. Ali said: What is this? They said: She committed indecency, and Umar ordered her stoning. He said: Send her back. So they sent her back, and he went to Umar and said: Do you not know that the pen is lifted from three: from the sleeper till he wakes, from the afflicted till he recovers, and from the child till he understands, or he said till he reaches puberty? He said: Yes. He said: Then what is with this one? He said: So he released her."
"11943","Ibn Jurayj reported: I said to Ata: A fool under interdiction? He said: His divorce, marriage, and sale are not valid."
"11944","From Mamar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, who said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to Adi ibn Adi al-Kindi: Whatever you overlook the fools in, do not overlook them in three: manumission, marriage, and divorce."
"11945","Ibn Jurayj reported: I was with Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz: Whatever you overlook the fools in, do not overlook them in divorce and manumission."
"11946","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, who was asked about the divorce of the delirious: he said, It is not valid until he understands."
"11947","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, and from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, who said: The divorce and manumission of the delirious are not valid, unless it is witnessed that he was sane at the time; otherwise he is made to swear; if he swears, well, otherwise it is valid against him."
"11948","From Mamar, from Qatada, about the mute who cannot speak: he said, His guardian divorces for him."
"11949","From al-Thawri, about the divorce of the mute, and I asked him: he said, He has no divorce except that he write? He said: And I have some doubt about it, even if he writes. He said: And his sale and purchase are not valid."
"11950","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: The divorce of the drunkard is valid; he is not like the sick man overpowered in mind; he brought what he brought while knowing he says what is improper, and he knows it."
"11951","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin — he heard them both saying: The divorce of the drunkard is valid, and he is flogged."
"11952","From Mamar, from Qatada, who said: His divorce is valid, and he is flogged."
"11953","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: His divorce and manumission are valid; his purchase, sale, and marriage are not valid."
"11954","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, who said: Divorce is valid for the drunkard because he drinks wine though Allah forbade it; his gift and charity are not valid."
"11955","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, when he was governor over Medina, validated the divorce of the drunkard. Ubaydullah ibn Ayman said: A man divorced his wife Ramla bint Tariq, and Muawiya validated it against him."
"11956","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Shabi and Ibrahim, who said: The divorce and manumission of the drunkard are valid."
"11957","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Harmala, from Ibn al-Musayyib, who said: The divorce of the drunkard is valid."
"11958","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, who said: The divorce of the drunkard is valid."
"11959","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Dawud, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, who said: Whatever the drunkard commits in his drunkenness is enforced against him."
"11960","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, from Tawus, who said: The divorce of the drunkard is nothing."
"11961","From a man, from Yahya ibn Said, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, that he used to say: The divorce of the drunkard is not valid."
"11962","From Ibn Abi Dhib, from al-Zuhri, from Aban ibn Uthman ibn Affan, that he said: The divorce of the drunkard and the idiot is not valid. Abd al-Razzaq said: And Abd al-Wahhab mentioned it from al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Dhib."
"11963","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: The divorce of the drunkard is not valid."
"11964","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Muslim ibn al-Dayyal, from Ibn Shubruma, who said: The divorce of the drunkard is valid; as for his marriage, I do not know, perhaps it is not valid. He said: And Ibn Layla said: His marriage and divorce are valid."
"11965","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata, who said: The divorce of the boy is valid when he reaches the point of attaining women."
"11966","From Mamar, from Qatada, and from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, about the child: they said, His divorce and manumission are not valid, and the penalties are not carried out upon him until he reaches puberty. Mamar said: And one who heard al-Hasan told me he said the like of al-Zuhris saying."
"11967","From al-Thawri, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Shabi, who said: The divorce of the child is nothing until he reaches puberty."
"11968","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim, who said: They did not consider the divorce of minors anything."
"11969","From al-Hasan ibn Umara, from al-Hakam, from Yahya ibn al-Jazzar, from Ali, that he did not consider the divorce of children anything."
"11970","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, from Husayn ibn Abdillah, from his grandfather, from Ali, who said: No divorce is binding upon the boy until he reaches puberty."
"11971","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib: Umar and Uthman ruled about the missing man that his wife waits four years, and four months and ten days after that, then she marries; if her first husband comes, he is given the choice between the dower and his wife."
"11972","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata al-Khurasani told me that Ibn Shihab told him that Umar and Uthman ruled about the inheritance of the missing: it is divided from the day four years pass for his wife, and she begins her waiting period of four months and ten days."
"11973","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Amr ibn Dinar told me that Umar ordered the guardian of the one absent from her to divorce her."
"11974","From al-Thawri, from Yunus ibn Khabbab, from Mujahid, about the lost one who was lost: he said, I entered the ravine and the jinn captivated me; my wife waited four years, then came to Umar; he ordered her to wait four years from when she raised her matter to him, then called her guardian who divorced, and ordered her to observe four months and ten days. He said: Then I came after she had married, and Umar gave me the choice between her and the dower I had given."
"11975","From Mamar, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, who said: A woman lost her husband and waited four years, then mentioned her matter to Umar ibn al-Khattab; he ordered her to wait four years from when she raised her matter to him; if her husband came, well, otherwise she married after the four years, having heard no mention of him. Then her husband came after that; while he was at his door seeking entry, or while he was going to his family, it was said: Your wife married after you. He asked about that and was told the news of his wife. He came to Umar ibn al-Khattab and said: Judge for me against one who wronged me over my family and stood between me and them. Umar was startled at that and said: Who is this? He said: You, Commander of the Faithful. He said: How? He said: The jinn took me away and I wandered in the land; I came and my wife had married; they claim you ordered her to that. Umar said: If you wish, we return your wife to you, and if you wish, we marry you to another. He said: Yes, marry me to another. Umar began asking him about the jinn while he informed him."
"11976","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Dawud ibn Abi Hind told me, from a man, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Umar ibn al-Khattab: A man of the Ansar went out to his peoples mosque to attend Isha and vanished; his wife came to Umar and mentioned that to him; he called her people and asked them about it, and they confirmed her. He ordered her to wait four years, then she came to him after they ended, and he ordered her and she married. Then her husband arrived and shouted at Umar: My wife — I neither divorced nor died. He said: Who is this? They said: The man whose affair was so-and-so. He said: Give him the choice between his wife and the dower. He asked him, and he said: A tribe of unbelieving jinn took me and I was among them. He said: What was your food among them? He said: What Allahs name was not mentioned over, and beans, until a Muslim tribe raided them and defeated them and found me among the captives. They said: What is your religion? I said: Islam. They said: You are upon our religion; if you wish, stay with us, and if you wish, we return you to your people. I said: Return me. They sent men with me; at night they talked with me and I with them, and by day it was the dust-wind, I followed it until I was returned to you. Ibn Jurayj said: As for Abu Qazaa, I heard him saying that Umar asked him: Where were you? He said: Unbelieving jinn took me and kept circling me about the earth until I fell upon a household among whom were Muslims, and they took me and returned me. He said: What of our food do they share in? He said: What Allahs name is not mentioned over of it, and what falls. Umar said: If I can, nothing shall fall from me."
"11977","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Yahya ibn Said told me he heard Ibn al-Musayyib saying: Umar ibn al-Khattab ruled about the woman who loses her husband and does not know what destroyed him, that she waits four years, then observes the waiting period of the widow, then marries if she wishes."
"11978","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Umar, who said: The wife of the missing waits four years."
"11979","From Mamar, from Ayyub, who said: Al-Walid wrote to al-Hajjaj to ask those with him about the missing man when he comes and his wife has married. Al-Hajjaj asked Abu Malih ibn Usama, and Abu Malih said: Suhayma bint Umar al-Shaybaniyya told me that she lost her husband in a campaign he fought and did not know whether he perished or not; she waited four years, then married. Her first husband came after she had married. She said: Her two husbands rode to Uthman and found him besieged; they asked him and mentioned their matter to him. Uthman said: In this situation? They said: It has occurred and must be. Uthman said: The first is given the choice between his wife and her dower. She said: Soon Uthman was killed, and they rode after till they came to Ali at Kufa and asked him. He said: In this situation? They said: What you see has occurred, and there must be a ruling in it. She said: And they told him Uthmans ruling. He said: I see for them only what Uthman said. The first chose the dower. She said: I helped my other husband with two thousand — the dower was four thousand — and he returned the mothers of children who had married after him, and returned their children with them."
"11980","From al-Thawri, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Ibn al-Musayyib, who said: If he is lost in the battle line, she waits a year; if lost otherwise, four years."
"11981","Ibn Jurayj reported from Ata, who said: She waits four years from the day she speaks, then his guardian divorces her, to take the surest course; and that divorce does not bar her husband from her if he comes and chooses her, the intent being that he take her back. She observes the waiting period of the widow. If he comes and chooses her, she chooses from the first. If he chooses her dower, she pays it from her wealth, and she does not observe a waiting period from the other; she remains with him as she is."
"11982","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who said: The husband is made to pay the dower. He said: As for us, we say: The woman pays it, and it is the more beloved of the two sayings to us."
"11983","From Mamar, from Qatada, who said: When four years pass from when the missing mans wife raises her matter, his wealth is divided among his heirs."
"11984","From Muhammad ibn Ubaydillah al-Arzami, from al-Hakam ibn Utayba: Ali said about the wife of the missing: She is a woman afflicted; let her be patient till death or divorce comes to her."
"11985","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from al-Hakam, from Ali, who said: She waits till she knows whether he is alive or dead."
"11986","From Mamar, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Hakam: Ali said: She is a woman afflicted; let her be patient till death or divorce comes to her."
"11987","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: It reached me that Ibn Masud agreed with Ali that she waits for him forever."
"11988","From Abu Hanifa, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, who said: She is a woman afflicted; let her be patient till death or divorce comes to her."
"11989","From al-Thawri, from Mughira, from Ibrahim, who said: She waits till she knows whether he is alive or dead."
"11990","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, who said: If he comes and finds her dead: he said, Her inheritance is his outright. Ata said: She is of him and he is of her, if she was married in his lifetime."
"11991","From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Karim, who says what Umar says: He is made to swear by Allah which he would have chosen had he found her alive — her or her dower."
"11992","From Mamar, from al-Hasan and Qatada, who said: If the missing comes and finds her dead with her husband, her inheritance belongs to the first, not the other; and she has her dower from the other for what was made lawful of her."
"11993","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: The first came and found her married, and her other husband died — does he still choose? He said: Yes. I said: Then the first died knowing that — do his heirs take his dower of her, then she does not inherit him? He said: I do not know."
"11994","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan saying, about a woman who lost her husband and married, then her other husband died, then the first came: he said, She returns her inheritance from the other to the others family, and she is the wife of the first. Mamar said: And Qatada said: She inherits the other; if the first died before coming, she inherits him too, and observes two waiting periods from them both."
"11995","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Shabi, who was asked about a woman who lost her husband, then married, then her other husband died, then the first came: he said, She returns the inheritance of the other, and she is the wife of the first; she inherits him and he inherits her."
"11996","From al-Thawri, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Shabi, that he said about a woman who lost her husband and married another, then he divorced her thrice, whether the other may marry her? He said: Yes; his divorce after counts not even a dung."
"11997","From Mamar, from al-Hasan and Qatada, about the missing whose wife married while he was alive, then the missing died while his wife was with her other husband: she has her dower for what was made lawful of her, she inherits the first, she observes from this other the waiting period of divorce, and observes from the first the waiting period of the widow. Qatada said: And this separation from the other counts as a divorce."
"11998","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, about a man reported dead to his wife, and she married, then the news reached the first and he divorced her: he said, She is forbidden to the other; she observes three courses, then is separated from them both. If she was pregnant and delivered after a month, she observes two months from the first, then is separated from them both. Maintenance is upon the one from whose fluid she observes. If she was pregnant and delivered after a month, she returns to the father of the pregnancy his maintenance, and maintenance falls upon the one who divorced her, and the waiting period from him is the remaining two months. When she has observed three months she is cleared from the first, his waiting period from her ended, and she observes from the other the remainder of the pregnancy; if he wishes to marry her in her waiting period, he may."
"11999","From Jabir, from al-Shabi, about the slave who flees while having a wife: he said, She is his wife till he dies. He said: And Khalid said from al-Hasan: If he flees, it is a separation. And al-Shabis saying is dearer to me."
"12000","From Ubaydillah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, who said: Umar wrote to the commanders of the troops: Summon so-and-so, people who cut off from Medina and left it, either to return to their wives, or to send them maintenance, or to divorce and send the maintenance of what passed."
"12001","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, who said: Umar wrote to his governors about the one absent from his wife and sending no maintenance. He wrote: Summon them, and order them to maintain or divorce; if they do not divorce, take them for the maintenance of what passed and what is to come."
"12002","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: If she took debt, it is upon him; what she ate from her wealth is not upon him."
"12003","From Mamar, from Mansur, from al-Nakhai, who said: If she took debt, he is held to it till he pays for her; if she did not borrow, she has nothing upon him if she ate from her wealth. Mamar said: And I asked Ibn Shubruma about it? He said: From the day she complains to the neighbours, maintenance is taken from then. Mamar said: And others say: From the day she raises her matter to the ruler."
"12004","From Abu Hanifa, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, who said: Whatever she took as debt is upon him. Abu Hanifa said: And we do not say that; he says: She has nothing unless the ruler fixes it."
"12005","From al-Thawri, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Shabi, who said: A woman came to Shurayh and said: My husband was absent, and I borrowed a dinar and spent on myself. He said: Did he order you to that? She said: No. He said: Then pay your debt."
"12006","From al-Thawri, from Mutarrif, from al-Shabi, who said: The disobedient wife has no maintenance? He says: When she disobeys her husband and goes out without his permission."
"12007","From al-Thawri, from Mutarrif, from al-Shabi, who said: If the woman is detained due to herself, she has no maintenance."
"12008","Ibn Jurayj reported: I asked Ata about the woman who does not find with the man what suffices her of maintenance? He said: She has only what he finds; he need not divorce her."
"12009","From Mamar, who said: I asked al-Zuhri about the man who does not find what to maintain his wife with — are they separated? He said: He is given time, and they are not separated. He recited: Allah burdens no soul beyond what He gave it; Allah will bring ease after hardship. Mamar said: And it reached me from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz the like of al-Zuhris saying."
"12010","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Said, from Ibn al-Musayyib, who said: If the man does not find what to maintain his wife with, he is compelled to separate from her. Al-Thawri said: And we do not take this saying; it is an affliction she was tried with, let her be patient."
"12011","From Ibn Uyayna, from Abu al-Zinad, who said: I asked about the man who does not find what to maintain his wife with? He said: They are separated. He said: I said: Sunna? He said: Yes, sunna."
"12012","From Mamar, from Hammad, who said: If the man does not find what to maintain his wife with, they are separated."
"12013","From Mamar, from Qatada, who said: If the man does not find what to maintain his wife with, they are separated."
"12014","From Mamar, from Qatada, who said: The woman is not kept in deprivation."
"12015","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, who said: If a man saw ten men fornicating with his wife, she is not forbidden to him."
"12016","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, from Ata and Mujahid, who said: If the woman fornicates, if he wishes he keeps her."
"12017","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Ibn Sirin, who said: Let him not approach her; let him separate from her."
"12018","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Zayd ibn Uthay, who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said to Abu Bakr: What if you found a man with your wife? He said: I would strike him with the sword. Then he said the like to Umar, and he said the like. Then the people followed upon the saying of Abu Bakr and Umar. Then he asked Suhayl ibn Bayda. He said: I say: May Allah curse you, for you are wicked, and may Allah curse you, for you are wicked, and may Allah curse the first of the three of us who reports this hadith. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: You interpreted, O Ibn Bayda."
"12019","From Mamar, from Harun ibn Riab, from Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr, who said: A man said: Messenger of Allah, my wife is of ill repute, and by Allah she does not withhold the hand of a toucher. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Divorce her. He said: Messenger of Allah, were I to part from her thrice? He said: Then enjoy your family."
"12020","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from a man, from a freedman of Banu Hashim: A man asked the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him: My wife does not withhold the hand of a toucher. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, ordered him to part from her. He said: She pleases me. He said: Then enjoy her."
"12021","Ibn Jurayj told me from Ata, who said: I said: The man accuses, and admits he had intercourse with her in the purity in which he saw upon her what he saw, and before he saw upon her what he saw. He said: He performs lian with her, and the child belongs to her."
"12022","From Mamar, from Qatada, who said: If he accuses his wife, he performs lian with her, whether he admitted he had intercourse or not."
"12023","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: What if he denies him after she delivers him? He said: He performs lian with her, and the child belongs to her. I said: Did not the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, say: The child belongs to the bed, and the stone for the adulterer? He said: Yes; that was only because people in Islam claimed children born on the beds of men, saying: They are ours. So the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: The child belongs to the bed, and the stone for the adulterer."
"12024","Ibn Jurayj reported: I said to Ata: Then he denied him after he reached puberty? He said: He is flogged, and she is his wife; that is only soon after she delivers. And I say: If he acknowledged her son and did not deny him, he has no denial even if she has not delivered."
"12025","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, who was asked about a man whose wife bore a child and he acknowledged him, then denied him after: he said, He is attached to him if he acknowledged him and he was born on his bed. He said: The lian that occurred in the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, was only that he said: I saw indecency upon her. Then al-Zuhri mentioned the Fazari hadith. He said: Said ibn al-Musayyib told me from Abu Hurayra, who said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and said: My wife bore a black boy — at the time implying he would deny him. The Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said: Have you camels? He said: Yes. He said: What are their colors? He said: Red. He said: Is among them a grey? He said: Yes, among them are grey ones. He said: How is that, do you think? He said: I do not know; perhaps a strain pulled it. He said: And this one, perhaps a strain pulled him. And he did not permit him to disown him."
"12026","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim, about the one who disowns his child after acknowledging: If he acknowledged for a moment, he is his child; if he denies after that, it is outright slander; he performs lian, and his child whom he had acknowledged is attached to him."
"12027","From Uthman, from Said, from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim, who said: If he acknowledged, then denied him: he said, The child binds him by the ruling of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, and he performs lian by the Book of Allah the Mighty and Majestic."
"12028","From al-Mujalid, from al-Shabi, from Umar, who said: If he acknowledged his child even for an hour, then denied after, he is attached to him."
"12029","From Ibn Jurayj, that it reached him that Shurayh said about the man who acknowledges his child then denies: He performs lian. That reached Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he wrote to him: If he acknowledged him for the blink of an eye, he may not deny him."
"12030","From Ibn Uyayna, from al-Mujalid, from al-Shabi, who said: If the man acknowledges his child, then disowns him, that is not his; he is attached to him though unwilling. And Amir said: I saw Shurayh do that with a man of Kinda: he acknowledged his child, then denied him, so he attached him to him. Then he turned to us and said: If this were so, any of you would soon disown his child."
"12031","From Uthman, from Said, from al-Hasan and Qatada, about the man who acknowledges his child then denies him: he said, He performs lian with her, and the child goes to her while his mother is with him. He mentioned from Hammad, from Ibrahim, who said: If he acknowledged a child of sixty years, then accused her, he performs lian with her and the child is bound to her. And Uthman said it too."
"12032","From Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim, who said: He performs lian by the Book of Allah the Mighty and Majestic, and the child binds him by the ruling of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him."
"12033","From al-Thawri, who said: If a woman was pregnant and her husband said: What is in her belly is not mine, he does not perform lian till she delivers, because he does not know whether there is a child in her belly or not; if he accused her of adultery, he performs lian."
"12034","From Mamar, who said: I said to al-Zuhri: What if a woman fornicated and said her child is not her husbands, and the husband said: Rather he is mine? He said: He is his if he acknowledges him."
"12035","Ibn Jurayj reported: I said to Ata: The umm walad of Maysara, freedman of Ibn Ziyad, claims her child is not from Maysara. He said: No; the child belongs to the bed, and the stone for the adulterer. Ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr said to him: Should the qafa experts be called for him? He said: The child belongs to the bed, and the stone for the adulterer. Ibn Jurayj said: And I say: If the free woman says it, she is disbelieved and beaten."
"12036","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and Qatadah, they said: If a man divorces once or twice, then accuses his wife, he performs li'an with her; if he makes her divorce final then accuses her, he is flogged and the child is attached to him. Qatadah said: If the waiting period ends in the case of one divorce, he is flogged and the child is attached to him."
"12037","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, about a man who accused his wife of adultery then divorced her as a hurtful act: He said: He performs li'an with her, because he accused her while she was his wife. And Ma'mar reported from al-Zuhri: He is flogged and the child is attached to him."
"12038","From Uthman ibn Sa'id, from Qatadah, from Jabir, from Ibn Abbas, he said: If he divorces her once or twice, then accuses her, he is flogged and there is no li'an between them. And Ibn Umar said: He performs li'an if he still holds the right of return."
"12039","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: If he accused her and she did not know until he divorced her thrice, he is given the hadd and the child is attached to him."
"12040","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, about a man who accused his wife then divorced her, and she did not know until her waiting period ended, he said: He is flogged and there is no li'an."
"12041","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: If he accuses his wife and he has no right of return, then he performs li'an if he holds the right of return; if he does not hold the right of return, he is beaten and the child is attached to him."
"12042","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ali and Ibn Mas'ud said: If he accused her while he had divorced her but retained return over her, he performs li'an with her; if he accused her after divorcing her finally, he does not perform li'an with her."
"12043","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, about a man who accuses his wife then divorces her, he said: No beating and no li'an. He said: And al-Hakam said: Beating. And Jabir reported from al-Sha'bi: He performs li'an."
"12044","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, that al-Harith ibn Yazid al-Ukli said to al-Sha'bi: He does not perform li'an; yet I am shy, when I see the truth, not to return to it."
"12045","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If he does not hold the right of return, he is beaten and the child is attached to him, and there is no li'an between them."
"12046","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: One who heard al-Hasan informed me, he says: If a man accuses then divorces thrice, he said: Bind him to what he fled from, he said: He performs li'an with her."
"12047","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, about the woman ransomed by khul': If he accused her before she ransomed herself from him, he is flogged and there is no li'an."
"12048","From al-Thawri, about a man who accuses his wife before she is brought to him, he said: He performs li'an with her."
"12049","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from al-Sha'bi, he said: If he accuses his wife before consummating with her, she gets half the dower if he performs li'an with her."
"12050","From al-Thawri, from Mutarrif, from al-Hakam, he said: If he accuses his wife before consummating with her, and she is pregnant, she gets the full dower and he performs li'an with her."
"12051","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri and Hammad, about a man who accused his wife before she was brought to him, they said: If she was pregnant he performs li'an with her, and they are separated, and she gets her full dower, and the child is hers. Ma'mar said: And Qatadah said: She gets half the dower, and he performs li'an with her if he had not consummated with her."
"12052","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man accuses his wife before she is brought to him, he said: He performs li'an with her and the child is his. And Amr said it too."
"12053","From Ma'mar, from Khusayf, from al-Sha'bi, he said: If a man accuses his wife while he is in a distant land, and he had not consummated with her, then he is flogged."
"12054","From al-Thawri, he said: If a man accuses his wife while he is in a distant land, he said: When he comes he performs li'an."
"12055","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, I said: If he says to his wife: I did not find you a virgin, and I do not say that from adultery, then he is not flogged. Umar did not flog; they claimed that ablution and the like remove virginity."
"12056","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, about a man saying to his wife: I did not find you a virgin, he said: Nothing is upon him; menstruation and leaping remove virginity."
"12057","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: Virginity is removed by other than intercourse, and there is no li'an between them."
"12058","From Ma'mar, from al-Hakam ibn Aban, he said: I asked Salim ibn Abdillah about that, and he said: Menstruation and leaping remove virginity."
"12059","From al-Thawri, from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from al-Sha'bi, about a man saying to his wife: I did not find you a virgin, he said: He is not beaten unless he charges her with adultery, because menstruation and vice remove virginity."
"12060","From Abdillah ibn Kathir, from Shu'bah, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, about a man who consummates with a woman and did not find her a virgin, he said: Virginity goes away from leaping and childbirth."
"12061","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, about a man to whom twins are born in one belly, and he disowns one and acknowledges the other, he said: He disowns both together, or claims both together. Sufyan said: And its explanation with us: if he disowned by the first and acknowledged the other, he is beaten and both are attached to him; if he acknowledged the first and disowned the other, he performs li'an and both are joined to him."
"12062","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: The li'an that took place in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was only that he said: I saw indecency upon her."
"12063","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A man says to his wife: O adulteress, and he says: I did not see that upon her, or without pregnancy, he said: He does not perform li'an with her. He said: And some say: There is no li'an except for pregnancy, or he says: I saw."
"12064","From al-Thawri, he said: If he says to her: O adulteress, he performs li'an with her in every case, when they are brought before the authority, whether he saw it or not, whether blind or not blind. Allah Almighty said: And those who accuse their wives."
"12065","From al-Thawri, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Ibrahim, he said: If a man accuses his wife and they do not bring it forth, she is his wife."
"12066","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, about a man who accused his wife then died before she raised him to the authority, he said: If she wishes she does not raise him to the authority, and she is his wife."
"12067","From Ma'mar, from al-Hasan, he said: She raises him to the authority, necessarily. He said: And it is the saying of Qatadah."
"12068","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Ayyub, from al-Sha'bi, about a man who accused his deaf-mute wife, he said: She is in the position of the dead; I beat him. And another said: I do not beat him until she expresses herself."
"12069","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: One of them died and they had not performed li'an, he said: The other inherits him."
"12070","From al-Thawri, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, about a man who accuses his wife then one of them dies, he said: They inherit each other, and there is no li'an between them. From Ma'mar, from Hammad, the like. From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, the like. Ma'mar said: And al-Hasan said it too, he said: They inherit each other, and the survivor is not asked about anything."
"12071","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, he said: If a man accuses his wife then dies before performing li'an with her, then if she confesses, she inherits her husband and is stoned; if she testifies, she inherits her husband and is not stoned; if she dies and he brings four witnesses testifying, he inherits her; if he testifies he is not flogged and does not inherit; if the husband confesses he is flogged and inherits; if he dies without testifying or confessing, he is not flogged and does not inherit. Qatadah said: Had he kept silent he would be in her position; he is not flogged and does not inherit."
"12072","From Abdillah ibn Kathir, from Shu'bah, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, the like of the saying of al-Thawri from Ibrahim: They inherit each other and there is no li'an between them. Al-Hakam said: And al-Sha'bi said: He performs li'an after death. And al-Hakam said: He is flogged and inherits her, if he accused her then she died."
"12073","From al-Thawri, from al-Sha'bi, he said: If a man accuses his wife while she is alive, he performs li'an with her; if he accuses her after she dies, he is flogged the hadd."
"12074","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, about a man who accused his wife before marrying her, then married her and she raised him to the authority, he said: He is flogged and does not perform li'an with her, and she is his wife."
"12075","From al-Thawri, he said: He is beaten for her, because the hadd became incumbent on him before he married her."
"12076","From Ma'mar, from Hammad, he said: If he belies himself before completing all her li'an, he is flogged and takes her back."
"12077","From al-Thawri, he said: If he belies himself after something remains of the li'an, he is beaten and she is his wife."
"12078","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: I said to him: What if the one who accuses his wife retracts before performing li'an with her, he said: She is his wife, and he is flogged."
"12079","From a man of Qays, from Abu Hanifah, he said: If a man accuses his wife then belies himself before performing li'an with her, he is flogged eighty and the child is attached to him, and they remain on their marriage; if he accuses her after being flogged, belying himself, there is no li'an between them, but he is flogged whenever he accuses her, because it is a testimony not accepted."
"12080","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: He retracted and belied himself after performing li'an with her, he said: He is not flogged. I said: Why? He said: They have separated; he has incurred the curse of Allah."
"12081","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: If he belies himself after performing li'an with her, he is flogged and the child is attached to him. Ma'mar said: And one who heard al-Hasan informed me, he says: He is flogged, and the child is not attached to him."
"12082","From Ma'mar, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Ibn al-Musayyab, that he heard him say: If the li'an husband repents and confesses after li'an, he is flogged, the child is attached to him, his wife is divorced an irrevocable divorce, he proposes to her along with the suitors, and that is whenever he belies himself."
"12083","From Ibn Jurayj, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Ibn al-Musayyab, that he heard him while being asked about the li'an husband when he confesses after his li'an: that he is flogged, and his wife is handed back to him."
"12084","From Ma'mar, from Hammad, he said: If he belies himself after completing his li'an, they are separated."
"12085","From al-Thawri and Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: The two who performed li'an never come together again."
"12086","From Qays ibn al-Rabi', from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah, from Abdillah ibn Mas'ud, he said: The two who performed li'an never come together again."
"12087","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: She is never lawful for him. He said: I did not see them wanting them to ever come together. He said: I said: Even if she marries another? He said: Yes."
"12088","From Qays ibn al-Rabi', from Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Ali, he said: The two who performed li'an do not come together."
"12089","From Abu Hashim, from al-Nakha'i, he said: If he belies himself, he is flogged and the child is attached to him, and they do not come together."
"12090","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: If he belies himself, they never marry each other again. Ma'mar said: And one who heard al-Hasan informed me, he says the like of al-Zuhri's saying."
"12091","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: If he belies himself, he is beaten with the hadd."
"12092","From Ma'mar, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: Whenever he belies himself he is flogged, and he proposes to her along with the suitors."
"12093","From Abu Hanifah, he said: Li'an is an irrevocable divorce."
"12094","From al-Thawri, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Ibn al-Musayyab, he said: If he belies himself, he is flogged and she is returned to him."
"12095","From Ma'mar, from Dawud, he said: I heard Ibn al-Musayyab say: If the li'an husband repents and confesses after li'an, he is flogged and the child is attached to him, his wife is divorced an irrevocable divorce, he proposes to her along with the suitors, and that is whenever he belies himself."
"12096","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, he said: When: And those who accuse their wives, the verse, was revealed, Sa'd ibn Ubadah said: Now I look and a man is astride her thigh, and she kept staring until she persisted; if she leaves I gather witnesses, I will not gather them until he fulfills his need; if I tell you what I saw you will beat my back eighty. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the Ansar: Do you not hear what your chief said? They said: O Prophet of Allah, do not blame him, for none among us is more jealous than he; by Allah, he never married a woman except a virgin, nor ever divorced a woman so that any of us could marry her. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No, except the clear proof Allah mentioned. He said: Then a cousin of his was tried, Hilal ibn Umayyah, and he came and told the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he found a man upon his wife, so Allah Almighty revealed: And those who accuse their wives, the verse, to the truthful. When he testified four times, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Stop him, for it has become obligatory. Then he said to him: If you lied, repent. He said: No, by Allah, I am truthful. Then he proceeded upon the fifth, then she testified four testimonies by Allah that he is among the liars, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Stop her, for it has become obligatory. Then he said to her: If you lied, repent. She was silent a while then said: I will not disgrace my people all the day. Then she proceeded upon the fifth. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If she brings him thus, and brings him thus, he is for so-and-so. She brought him upon the disliked of that. Ma'mar said: It reached me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had Allah not revealed about him, I would have had a ruling in him."
"12097","From Ibrahim ibn Muhammad, he said: Dawud ibn al-Husayn informed me, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) separated the two who performed li'an when they performed li'an, and said: When she delivers, bring him to me before she nurses him. And he said: If she brings him black, curly-haired, she is cast at him; if she brings him red, straight-haired, he is from the woman's husband. She brought him black, curly-haired. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: His matter is not, were it not what Allah decreed in him."
"12098","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, about li'an and the sunnah in it upon the hadith of Sahl ibn Sa'd, brother of Banu Sa'idah, that a man of the Ansar came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, what of a man who finds a man with his wife, so he kills him and you kill him, or how should he act? Allah Almighty revealed about his case what is mentioned in the Quran of the matter of the two who perform li'an. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah has decreed about you and your wife. He said: They performed li'an in the mosque while I was present. He said: When they finished, he said: I lied against her, O Messenger of Allah, if I keep her. He divorced her thrice before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him, when they finished the li'an. He parted from her before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: That is the separation between every two who perform li'an. She was pregnant and he denied him, so her son was called after his mother."
"12099","Ibn Jurayj informed us, perhaps from al-Zuhri, from Sahl ibn Sa'd, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If she brings him reddish, thin, as though a gecko, then I see her only truthful and he lied against her; if she brings him black with buttocks, then I see him only truthful against her. She brought him upon the disliked of that."
"12100","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I heard Abdillah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr say: It was said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): He is this, O Messenger of Allah, her child. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gazed at him with his sight, until we saw he would say something to him, but he said nothing."
